<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BAL0008> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Śākyamuni Buddha Shrine (consecrated in 1957 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BAL0008"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "The Buddha statue enshrined in this small shrine is one of the biggest statues in Balambu. The shrine is located west of the street leading to the southern gateway to the city. The statue (which faces) east is constructed out of a single stone. The Buddha exhibiting the bhūmisparśamudrā is seated on a double-decked lotus throne placed on a stone plinth. Generally, Buddha statues are not adorned except for his classical features such as elongated ears, curly hairs, and half-closed eyelids. This motif is followed here too. An inscription below the statue states it was established by Śakhalāla, his wife Āsāmāyā and their two daughters in NS 1077 (1957 CE). Around a decade ago, iron railings were welded around the statue for its safekeeping. Around the same time, a roof was constructed above the statue and was capped by a metal caitya (local knowledge, Maharjana, male, 63 years). Pruscha (1975, 155) has published a photo of this statue before the railing was installed. Śakhalāla&rsquo;s family members take care of the statue. However, they do not perform any anniversary rituals. The members of the local communities worship the statue here on auspicious occasions, such as procession, festivals and so forth." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BAL0008> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BAL0008> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BKT0050> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Indrāyaṇī Pīṭha" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BKT0050"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Four aniconic stone statues representing various deities are established inside the Pīṭha. They are adorned with toraṇa , one of which is larger than the others. The local community worships it as the Indrāyaṇī. There is another kīrtimukhatoraṇa behind this statue and a large tree growing behind the Pīṭha. A phalcā and a hiti is constructed nearby the temple. Indrāyaṇī&rsquo;s statue, brought from the deity&rsquo;s god-house (dyaḥchẽ ), is exhibited here during auspicious occasions. Thrice every year, the deity&rsquo;s chariot procession is organised and is taken around the city quarters of the core city. The local community, especially from the western part of Bhaktapur, come here to worship on their birthdays and conduct kalaḥpūjā . Devotees throng to the temple on the eighth, ninth, and tenth day of Dasaĩ festival. Some of them also offer animal sacrifice during Dasaĩ." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BKT0050> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BKT0050> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0003> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Dabū and temporary shrine" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0003"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "There are many dab ūs (traditional platforms) in the old settlements of Kathmandu Valley. Most of them were made in the Malla period and were used for the performances of dramas and dances. An inscription found in Nārāyaṇacaura, Naksal from the reign of King Jayadeva the second (713-733 CE) mentions two platforms known as ponḍimaṇḍapikā and prekṣaṇamaṇḍapikā (D. Vajrācārya VS 2030, 569 and K. Tamo t 2013, 20). In the Malla period, many dances and dramas were performed in dabūs, especially during festivals and ceremonies ( K. Tamo t 2013, 20). Buṅgamatī's Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ) collapsed in the devastating earthquakes of 2015. Fortunately, at that time the statue of Buṅgadyaḥ normally enshrined there was on a chariot travelling from Buṅgadyaḥ towards Patan and so remained unharmed. Since the restoration of the temple is likely to last many years, a temporary shrine building has been built on the foundation of this dabū to house the deity. This shrine building measures 412 &times; 412 cm and was built using regular brick in mud mortar. It is covered by a roof made of steel trusses, plywood and metal sheets. There were two large stone lions guarding the northern entrance and two smaller lions in front of the southern entrance of the Karuṇāmaya Temple. These have been moved to stand in front of the dabū . A bell suspended from a stone frame was also moved from its original location to the left of the shrine&rsquo;s entrance. Following the 2015 collapse of the Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ) this temporary shrine was built to house the main deity of Buṅgadyaḥ (BUN0001-008). Also, two large stone lions (BUN0001-008), two small stone lions (BUN0001-009), and a bell suspended from a stone stand (BUN0001-010) were moved from the temple to the front of this temporary shrine. No sources can be found with which to date the initial construction of this dabū . Every 12 years, two dances are performed here: jalapyākh&atilde; (a dance from Harisiddhi) and gathupyākh&atilde; (a dance from Thecho)." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0003> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0003> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0004> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Taḥgaḥcībhāḥ ca. 16th cent." ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0004"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Taḥgaḥcībhāḥ Caitya (&quot;Big Caitya&quot;) is located at the south-west corner of Macchindrabāhāḥ. It is the biggest caitya in this area and exhibits a style typical of the 16th century ( Gutschow (2011, vol 1: 152). H. Śākya (1993, 82) describes it as a Vajradhātu caitya . The caitya is built with small traditional mā apā bricks which attests to its antiquity. The four corners, the frames around the niches and the finial are all made of stone. Statues of Akṣobhya, Ratnasaṃbhava, Amitābha and Amoghasiddhi are installed in the eastern, southern, western and northern niches of the caitya respectively. In addition, a statue of Akṣobhya is attached to the eastern plinth and an inscription has been placed on the south-eastern corner of the caitya . The inscription mentions that the caitya was renovated by five Śākyas in 1749 CE. A historical photo from 2000 (published in Bu ṅgamatīko Sā̃skṛtika Sampadāharū , p. 50) shows that the caitya was in a dilapidated condition. However, it was renovated in 2002. A Śākya family residing in the vicinity south of the caitya takes care of it and performs all annual rituals." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0004> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0004> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0005> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Bhagavān Degaḥ" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0005"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Bhagavān Degaḥ is located to the west of Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ). It was originally built as a two-tiered temple and later converted into a hybrid structure with a domed roof. Bhagavān Degaḥ is partly depicted in a watercolour by Henry Ambrose Oldfield , dated 1857. This painting serves as the sole documentation of the temple&rsquo;s restorations and reconfigurations over the past 150 years. No records of its initial construction could be found. It can be assumed that the temple was severely damaged in the 1934 earthquake and was later rebuilt in its current shape. The lower roof was preserved but the upper roof was replaced by a plastered brick dome with a gilt copper finial. Later, the lower roof was rebuilt with a brass sheet cover and decorative eaves. The original open pillar arcade was closed off with lattice woodwork and a door was installed in the east fa&ccedil;ade. This work was probably carried out some time between 1980-1990. The inner cella was partly covered with industrial tiles and a new door and a steel scissor gate have also been installed for security purposes. Today, only a few of the original components survive; these include the carved pillars, corbels and cross beams. Most of the edge stones and original tile floors have been lost. Until a few decades ago this temple was known as the Sṛṣṭikāntā Lokeśvara Temple. Even the 2002 book Cultural Heritage of Bungmati (in p. 53) refers to it as the temple of Sṛṣṭikāntā Lokeśvara. A statue of Śākyamuni Buddha was enshrined in this temple around four decades ago. Before this, the sanctum of the temple was empty. The temple's new name of Bhagavān Degaḥ is closely related to this new deity. Elderly people in Bungmati tell of a legend which links this temple with the Sṛṣṭikāntā Lokeśvara Temple of Nālā (in the Kabhrepalnacok district). According to this story, the idol of Sṛṣṭikāntā Lokeśvara (which is currently in Nālā) was originally to be found in this temple. Some time ago, the idol disappeared from the temple of Bungmati. Eventually, it was found lying in the mud somewhere. &lsquo; Nāla &rsquo; is a Newari word meaning mud. The deity found in the &lsquo; nāla&rsquo; was then established in its current location. Only the Pāneju Saṅgha of Bungmati have the right to perform the life-cycle (daśakarma ) rites of Sṛṣṭikāntā Lokeśvara in Nālā. A group of eight p ānejus from Bungmati goes to Nālā for this purpose every year on the full moon day of Phāguna. Like the temple of Nālā, this temple also faces east. Since the Buddha statue was established in the temple, two families of Śākyas and Tulādhara have taken on responsibility for performing the ritual worships. A procession now also takes place in Bungmati on the day of Buddha Jayantī (the full moon day of Vaiśākha). On this day, people place the deity on a cot and carry him around the surrounding area of Bungmati." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0005> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0005> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0008> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Licchavi Caitya" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0008"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This Caitya is located to the south-west of the Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ) of Bungmati. Bungamati is one of the oldest settlements in the Kathmandu Valley. This monument was constructed in the Licchavi period and stands testament to the antiquity of the settlement. The upper section of the Caitya was placed upon an unusually shaped brick and cement pedestal some time before 1990. Since then, the stone finial has separated from the structure and is now lying in pieces on top of the dome, which is also broken. The stone pavement around the Caitya was removed following the 2015 earthquake. The upper part of the Caitya has four small empty niches, each of which are surmounted by images of Kīrtimukha. Locals worship the Caitya during their morning visit to the temples. Besides that, other regular worships are not observed at this Caitya." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0008> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0008> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0010> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Licchavi Caitya and statue of Buddha" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0010"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This group of three objects is located to the west of Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ). The brick pedestal supports a massive stone slab (131cm x 62 cm), upon which the Licchavi Caitya, the Buddha statue and the dome fragment have been loosely placed. This Caitya was constructed some time in the Licchavi period and stands testament to the antiquity of the Bungamati settlement. The Licchavi Caitya is eroded, slightly damaged and missing its finial. Parts of the right hand and leg of the Buddha statue have broken off, most of the nimbus is missing and the damaged head has been patched up with cement mortar. The dome fragment is missing its finial and its lower part sits to the right of the statue. This collection of items was obviously quite recently assembled, and it is not known where the large stone base came from. Locals worship the Caitya during their morning visit to the temples. Besides that, other regular worships are not observed at this Caitya." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0010> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0010> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0011> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Caitya (1944 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0011"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This Caitya is located to the west of Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ) and exhibits a hybrid design. The bottom half features elements typical of a j vālāvali caitya such as lions at the corners of its base and a lotus pedastal, while the upper half resembles a ś ikharakūṭa caitya with its Lokeśvara statues and four Buddhas attached to the dome. The Caitya is in a good condition and has been cleaned recently. The inscription of the Caitya mentions that its construction was funded by Nararāja Pāneju in 1944 CE. Nararāja also established a guṭhī to perform the daily and anniversary ritual worship of the Caitya. However, his descendants sold their property and moved away from the locality, selling the guṭhī land and thus bringing an end to the daily worship. The Caitya's anniversary worship is performed on the full moon day of Āśvina." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0011> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0011> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0012> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Dharmadhātujinālaya Caitya (1958 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0012"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This Dharmadhātujinālaya Caitya is located at the south-west corner of the Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ). Shields above the Caitya's harmikā symbolise the four Tathāgatas: the jewel, lotus, viśvavajra and vajra . Below the dome, one can find statues of the four Tathāgatas: Vairocana (east), Ma&ntilde;juśrī (north), Vajrasattva (west) and Amitābha (south). In addition to this, four Tārās have been placed at the corners above three layers of lotus petals. The composite of dome and drum contains eight niches and rests in turn on the pericarp of a fully opened lotus flower with three circles of petals. The Four Buddhas have been placed on a throne atop some upward-opening single-layered lotus blossoms. Statues of Akṣobhya, Ratnasambhava, Amitābha and Amoghasiddhi have been placed on the eastern, southern, western and northern sides of the Caitya respectively. Akṣobhya&rsquo;s seat is decorated with images of two elephants and Maitreya. Similarly, the southern side is decorated with images of two horses and Vajrapāṇi, the western side displays peacocks and Padmapāṇi, and the northern side exhibits images of Garuḍas and Ma&ntilde;juśrī. The lower lotus is decorated with a circle of water motifs and rests upon still another socle. This socle takes a vimśatikoṇa shape and is decorated with vajra symbols. Below this, one can see a lion throne with four sculpted images lions at each of its four corners, as well as images of Caturmahārājas at each of the cardinal directions. Below the throne, one can see a vedikā and a plinth. This Caitya was commissioned by Harṣamuni Śākya in 1958. The anniversary ritual worship of the Caitya is performed on the full moon day of Vaiśākha." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0012> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0012> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0013> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Dharmadhātujinālaya Caitya (1983 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0013"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "The Caitya is located to the west of Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ) and is part of a group of four caityas and a dharmadhātu maṇḍala, all of which are aligned in a north-south direction. The area surrounding the caitya is paved with black flagstones and has been encroached upon by some of the building materials used for the reconstruction of the Mandira. Generally, caityas follow the pattern of placing statues of Akṣobhya to the east, Amoghasiddhi to the north, Amitābha to the west and Ratnasambhava to the south. However, this Dharmadhātujinālaya Caitya displays statues of Vairocana to the east, Ma&ntilde;juśrī to the north, Vajrasattva to the west and Amitābha to the south. This monument was constructed in 1983, and its anniversary worship is performed on the full moon day of Vaiśākha. Tradition dictates that an umbrella be placed on top of the caitya on the full moon day of Āśvina." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0013> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0013> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0014> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Dharmadhātujinālaya Caitya (1962 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0014"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "The caitya is located to the west of Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ) and is part of a group of four caityas and a dharmadhātu maṇḍala which are alligned in a north-south direction. The area surrounding the caitya is paved with black flagstone and has been encroached upon by building materials for the reconstruction of the Karuṇāmaya Mandira. Generally, caityas follow a pattern of placing statues of Akṣobhya to the east, Amoghasiddhi to the north, Amitābha to the west and Ratnasambhava to the south. However, this caitya has Vairocana facing east, Ma&ntilde;juśrī north, Vajrasattva west and Amitābha south. The caitya was built in 1962. A special worship was organised in the courtyard for the welfare of the community three months before its construction began. During the worship, donations were collected to fund its construction. On the full moon day of Āśvina an umbrella is placed on top of the caitya and on the full moon day of Vaiśākhaits the anniversary worship is performed." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0014> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0014> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0015> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Dharmadhātu Maṇḍala (1964 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0015"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This Dharmadhātu Maṇḍala is located to the west of Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ) and is part of a group of four caityas, all of which are aligned in a north-south direction. The area surrounding the Dharmadhātu Maṇḍala is paved with black flagstones and has been encroached upon by some of the building materials used for the reconstruction of the Karuṇāmaya Mandira. The Maṇḍala was constructed in 1964. The inscription does not mention the name of its founder, though locals state that his name was Harṣamuni Śākya." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0015> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0015> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0016> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Dharmadhātujinālaya Caitya (1964 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0016"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "The Caitya is located to the west of Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ) and is part of a group of four caityas and a dharmadhātu maṇḍala, all of which are alligned in a north-south direction. The area surrounding the Caitya is paved with black flagstones and has been encroached upon by some of the building materials for the reconstruction of the Karuṇāmaya Mandira. Generally, caityas follow a pattern of placing statues of Akṣobhya to the east, Amoghasiddhi to the north, Amitābha to the west and Ratnasambhava to the south. However, within this Dharmadhātujinālaya Caitya Vairocana is located to the east, Ma&ntilde;juśrī to the north, Vajrasattva to the west and Amitābha to the south." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0016> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0016> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0017> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Caitya (1964 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0017"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This Caitya is located to the west of Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ) and is part of a group of another four caityas and a dharmadh ātu ma ṇḍala, all of which are aligned in a north-south direction. The area surrounding the Caitya is paved with black flagstones and has been encroached upon by some of the building materials for the reconstruction of the Karuṇāmaya Mandira. The base structure of the Caitya is made from brick and plastered with cement mortar, however, the dome and finial are made of stone. Four Buddha statues have been placed in the niches that face each of the four cardinal directions. The Caitya was constructed in 1964. An umbrella is placed on top of the Caitya on the full moon day of Āśvina, and its anniversary worship takes place on the full moon day of Vaiśākha." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0017> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0017> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0022> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Bare Nani (17th cent.)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0022"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Bare Nani is located to the south-east of Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ) in Bungmati. This nani is also referred to as a bāhāḥ ( Locke 1985, 239) even though its architectural design does not much resemble this kind of structure . A pair of stone lions have been placed at its entrance, however the building lacks an āg&atilde;chẽ (a shrine of the esoteric clan deity) which is normally the main feature of a bāhāḥ . However, there is an āg&atilde;chẽ in one of the houses located at the south-eastern corner of the courtyard. Bare Nani is primarily related to the Śākyas who are priests in the Hayagrīva Mandira ( BUN0002 ) living nearby. The saṅgha of the Śākyas is also active; for example, the initiation rituals of local Śākya boys are performed in this nani . A vajradhātu caitya stands in the middle of the courtyard, and statues of the Four Buddhas have been placed around it with one at each of the four cardinal directions. The caitya has been covered with a thick lime paint that covers the motifs on the harmikā and trayodaśa-bhuvana . In front of the caitya, one can find a dharmadhātu maṇḍala and an Akṣobhya shrine, both of which were established in 1980. Local residents claim that this caitya was established before the Red (Rāto) Matsyendranātha was brought to the Kathmandu Valley. However, there is no evidence to support this claim. The caitya was repaired in 1659, and the courtyard was paved with bricks in 1682. This year also saw two lion statues placed at the entrance of the courtyard. A parasol is offered to the caitya every year on the full moon days of Āśvina and Caitra. A feast is also organised on these dates." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0022> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0022> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0023> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Gaṇeśa Shrine (c. mid- 17th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0023"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Cola Nani is located at the northern end of Bungamati. It consists of one Gaṇeśa shrine and three caityas . The shrine was built around 1973; people brought wood from the nearby forest for its construction. Before this, the Gaṇeśa shrine stood in an open space. Leaning against the back wall, Gaṇeśa's house is supported by eight plain columns and has a hip roof covered in tiles (which local residents call Chinese tiles). The Nani is also known as Gubhāḥkhyaḥ Nani due to the fact that Gubhāḥjus and Vajrācāryas live in its vicinity. Local residents say that Bandhudatta Ācārya helped King Narendradeva and Lalita Jyāpu to bring the Red Matsyendranātha to the Kathmandu Valley, and that he lived in this Nani. The Vajrācāryas living in this area therefore believe that they are the descendants of Bandhudatta." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0023> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0023> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0024> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Buṅga Bahī (18th cent.)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0024"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Buṅga Bahī, also known as Prathampura Mahāvihāra, is located at the northwestern end of Buṅgamatī. The monument can be reached from the northern gate of the Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ). This bahī is in Lalitpur but is not counted among the major bahīs of the district. It is named after Buṅgamatī, but is also referred to as Prathamapura Mahāvihāra, meaning the first bahī of the area. It is under the ownership of local Śākyas. These people write their last name as Brahmācārya Bhikṣu. The bahī has four entrances, one at each of its four sides, but the eastern one forms the main entrance. The bahī's hip roof is supported by plain struts, whilst the first floor is held up by double-layered pillars. As is often the case with bahī, one can find a phucā atop its roof. However, the śivaliṅga -shaped finial sets this bahī apart from others; these structures generally have a caitya -shaped finial. A veranda surrounds the first floor of the bahī complex. Inside, an L-shaped staircase leads up to the first floor. The sanctum of the bahī is on the first floor of the western building and houses an east-facing statue of Akṣobhya. Two stone lions and two metal flags flank the entrance of the sanctum. Carved panels depicting a Gandharva, a female figure and a water vessel have been fixed on either side of the entrance. A śivaliṅga -shaped aśoka caitya stands in the courtyard. No historical record could be found with which to date the bahī . However, a local folk tale tells that Matsyendranātha was brought to the Valley and requested to live in Bungmati. However, he replied that he did not want to dwell in a place without a bahī . As a result, this bahī was constructed and the deity accepted the request to live here. According to the bahī members, this bahī was established by Dhyānacandra Miśra. Three merchant brothers from Patan, Dharmaśrī Miśra, J&ntilde;ānaśrī Miśra and Dhyānacandra Miśra, spent the money they had earned through Tibetean trade to establish three bahīs . These are known as Kvanti Bahī, I Bahī and the present bahī. However, no historical record is available to verify this claim. The only inscriptions found in the bahī complex stem from the late Malla period. The bahī suffered from multiple earthquakes and has undergone reconstruction multiple times. This took place most recently in 2005 and was carried out by the Department of Archeology in Nepal. The main worship rituals that take place are the annual ritual worship of the caitya , a Vasundharā worship, a Mahākāla worship, and a srāddha (a ritual performed in honour of dead relatives) for Dhyānacandra Miśra. A guṭhī endowed with 45 ropanī of land in Bungmati and Sunaguthi was established to run the social and religious activities of the bahī ( Locke 1985 , 223). However, some of the guṭhī's lands have been privatised, and even the tenant farmers working the other lands no longer give the bahī's share to the guṭhī . The saṅgha -members of the bahī therefore rely on the collection of donations in order to fund these activities." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0024> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0024> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0025> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Gaṇeśa Mandira" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0025"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Gaṇeśa Mandira is located in Kotā Ṭola, Bungmati. The Tulādhara and Mālī caste groups worship this Gaṇeśa as their local deity. The mandira faces south and has a jhĩgaṭī tiled roof supported by carved struts. A metal pinnacle and parasol can be found atop the roof. A toraṇa has been placed above the carved wooden door. The main fa&ccedil;ade is decorated with latticed woodwork and two small latticed windows have been installed in the eastern and western walls of the temple. An aniconic stone image of Gaṇeśa with a metal crown on his head is enshrined in the sanctum. Besides the main shrine, many iconic and aniconic stone images can be seen around the sanctum. A wooden toraṇa is attached to the eastern wall. There was a hiti (stepwell) to the east of the temple until a few decades ago. However, it gradually turned into a rubbish dumping site and was eventually covered up with mud. Before the hiti was completely filled in, some of the statues were removed and relocated to this temple and the nearby Licchavi caitya (BUN0026) . The temple was reconstructed in 2003. The projecting roof on the northern side was left unfinished during the reconstruction process, meaning that the temple's structure appears unbalanced. Local residents traditionally come to this temple to worship Gaṇeśa before they begin any other auspicious rituals such as initiation ceremonies, marriage or jaṃko (Skt. bhīmarathārohaṇa ) . The Kusle (Kapālī) community were responsible for taking care of this temple until a few years ago. However, now that this community no longer takes care of this temple there are no official caretakers ( dyaḥpāḥlāḥs )." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0025> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0025> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0026> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Licchavi Caitya (9th century)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0026"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This Licchavi caitya is located to the north of the street that leads to Karuṇamaya Temple ( BUN0001 ) just after passing the Dey Pukhu. It is built on top of a square base measuring 90 x 90 cm and with total height of 145 cm. Only the upper part of the caitya seems to be from the original Licchavi structure. Stone slabs have been placed aroud the base of the caitya, forming a roof-like form over the square base and the stone statues in its walls. H. Śā kya (1993, 72) refers to this monument as Caturdvāreṣu Caityāṅkita Caitya. A few decades ago, a functioning hiti (stone spout) could be found to the east of the Gaṇeśa Mandira (BUN0025) of the Kotā Ṭola. Later, water stopped flowing from the spout and it fell into disuse. Now, the area is full of refuse, and the spout area has been covered with mud. Before it was filled in, a Licchavi period caitya , a statue of Bhagīratha, the original spout and some other statues were moved away from the hiti to their present locations. Bhagīratha&rsquo;s statue and the spout have been fixed to the new hiti, which is located in front of the Gaṇeśa Mandira. Bangdel (1995, 358) dates the Bhagīratha statue to the 9th century. A statue of Umāmaheśvara (which is dated by Bangdel (1995, 357) to the 9th century) has been placed on the western side of the present caitya . Its western niche remains empty. According to local residents, there was a statue of Nārāyaṇa in the niche. However, this was stolen around 1994. The northern niche of the caitya has a deteriorated statue which is thought to be Śivapārvatī. A Licchavi caitya has been joined to the upper part of the Caturdvāreṣu Caityāṅkita Caitya. The upper caitya has small caitya -shaped objects in its four niches. The garbha has been damaged in many places, and has been joined to the niche and the harmikās with cement. To the east of this caitya, one can find a dharmadhātujinālaya caitya which was established here in 1956. Local residents worship this caitya whilst visiting other shrines in the morning." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0026> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0026> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0028> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Dharmadhātujinālaya Caitya" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN0028"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "The Dharmadhātujinālaya Caitya is located at Kotā Ṭola to the north of Dey Pukhu. Its vedikā stands on a rectangular platform. Above the vedikā one can see a lion throne that displays sculpted images of four lion figures and Caturmahārājas on the four corners and at each of the cardinal directions, respectively. The Four Buddhas have been placed on a throne located on the upward opening single-layer lotus blossoms. Statues of Akṣobhya, Ratnasambhava, Amitābha and Amoghasiddhi are located on the eastern, southern, western and northern faces of the caitya respectively. Akṣobhya&rsquo;s seat is decorated with images of two elephants and Maitreya. Similarly, the south side shows two horses and Vajrapāṇi, the west side displays peacocks and Padmapāṇi, and the north side contains images of Garuḍas and Ma&ntilde;juśrī. Three layers of lotus petals can be found above the Buddha statues and small statues of the Four Buddhas and Four Tārās are placed above these petals. Cement plaster has been used to join the caitya's garbha and harmikā . The yaḥsĩ has also been plastered with cement. The structure of the caitya suggests that it can be dated back to the 19-20th century. According to the local residents, it was built by a Tulādhara family. Remnants of a collapsed phalcā lie to the north of the caitya . Local residents assume that a stone inscription is buried somewhere in this rubble." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN0028> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN0028> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN4004> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Āratī Chẽ" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN4004"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Āratī Chẽ, a ceremonial rest house (capāḥ), is closely related to Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ) and located to the temple's north-west. The building's name translates as &quot;oil lamp house&quot; meaning &quot;the building where oil lamps are prepared&quot;. According to local informants, the original Āratī Chẽ collapsed in the 1934 earthquake and was not reconstructed until 2018. A photo taken in 1968 (published in Bungmati 1968: A Survey by Danish Architect Students , page 21) shows the building site to be empty. The 2018 reconstruction was financed by the Lalitpur Municipality and represents a hybrid structure combining traditional and modern styles and building techniques. The walls have been constructed using regular brick and are painted with surkhi (brick dust and lime mortar). Horizontal and vertical timber bands have been inserted at regular intervals as seismic strengthening devices, something that is not a traditionally found in this type of building. A concrete slab supports the open terrace. The building's main facade (which faces south) has been built in a traditional configuration whereas the facades facing west and north remain unfinished and lack the traditional overhanging roofs. Oil lamps ( āratī ) are prepared at Aratī Chẽ for ceremonial use at the Karuṇāmaya temple. The deity Buṅgadyaḥ (also known as Karuṇāmaya) is worshipped four times a day: in the early morning, at noon, in the evening and in the middle of the night. Oil lamps are offered to the deity at the end of each worship (see Locke 1985, 238 for more details). The Aratī Chẽ guṭhī is managed by a Śākya family, one of the 31 priest families of Karuṇāmaya Mandira. This family prepares the oil lamps and brings them to the temple. In the time between the building's destruction in 1934 and its reconstruction in 2018, the family prepared the oil lamps in their house." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN4004> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN4004> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN4005> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Bāsimali Phalcā (1868 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN4005"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Bāsimali Phalcā is located to the northwest of Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ) and lies between Dhvākhā Phalcā and Āratī Chẽ. There are more than half a dozen phalcās in the courtyard of this temple. Many of the social-cultural activities related to Karuṇāmaya are organised in the locality, and these phalcās were constructed as sites within which to hold such activities. This three-bayed phalcā is located on the ground floor of a three-storey building. The roof of the building is supported by six carved struts and is covered with corrugated metal sheets. The second storey has a three-bayed window. The construction of this phalcā was facilitated by a Tāmrākāra family from Cāsukhela, Bhaktapur in 1868 CE. Originally, the building was called satala. Its founders had established a guṭhī for its management. However, the building collapsed in the 1934 earthquake and was reconstructed by the desecendents of the founders in 1942. A historical photo taken around 1968 shows that the building once had a jhĩgaṭī -tiled roof and a traditional window ( Bungamati 1968, 21). The Bāsimali Phalcā collapsed again in 2001 and in the same year Nārāyaṇadevī Tāmrākāra, another descendent of the founders, took on the task of renovating it. Once the renovations were complete, she deposited a lump sum of money in the bank to allow for the continuation and management of bhajana chanting in Bāsimali Phalcā. A statue of Vāsuki Nāga and a lion are attached to the inner wall of the phalcā . Locals worship Vāsuki as Viṣṇu and the lion as Garuḍa. Nārāyaṇadevī Tāmrākāra also donated musical instruments to a bhajana group in 2001, and this group now performs bhajana during the months of Gũlā and Kārttika. A group of yogin returns here after taking part in the Hariśaṅkara procession of Pharping before proceeding on to the Paśupatinātha Temple to perform Jogīcakra Pūjā. Upon returning from Pharping, the yogins carry a thick roṭī on their head which is tied and fixed in place by knots made by their matted hair. They rest for a while in Bāsimali Phalcā and offer the roṭīs to Karuṇāmaya. Afterwards, they distribute the remaining roṭī to the public as the gift of the deity. Some of the locals claim that the stale mali (Nep. ro ṭī ) was distributed in the phalcā, and that this is how the monument got its name." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN4005> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN4005> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN4007> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Tāhā Phalcā" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/BUN4007"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Tāhā Phalcā is located to the north-east of the Karuṇāmaya Mandira ( BUN0001 ). It derives its name from the Newari term &lsquo; t āhā &rsquo; , which means &ldquo;long&rdquo;. It was severely damaged in the 2015 earthquakes, which led to it being dismantled and reconstructed in 2017. The five-bayed Phalcā has six carved wooden posts. Two-thirds of the building have a flat slab roof whilst the remaining part is covered by a hip roof tiled with traditional pvala apā . Thirty-seven struts have been fixed in place to support the roof. The walls of the building are made of desi apā (modern bricks). There are four latticed windows on the first floor and seven on the second floor. Tāhā Phalcā is used to organise many social-cultural activities related to the deity. During the months of Guṃlā and Kārttika, various groups from Bungmati come to chant bhajanas in the Phalcā. The Jyāpu community from Lāchi Ṭola are particularly common amongst the chanters who visit this Phalcā. The upper storey of the building is mostly used to organise feasts, though many other social gatherings are also organised in its rooms." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/BUN4007> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/BUN4007> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KAT0930> a hg:DhungeDhara ;
    rdfs:label "Bhindyaḥ Hiti" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KAT0930"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Bhindyaḥ Hiti is located next to the Bhīmasena Mandira in Bhīmasenasthāna Ṭola, Kathmandu. Dissimilar to many other hitis in the Kathmandu Valley, Bhindyaḥ Hiti has only one water spout, facing east. A makara motif is carved on the conduit of the hiti and a statue of Vidhyādhara and Umāmaheśvara are respectively installed below and above the conduit. Add to this, statues of Bhagīratha, Gaṇeśa, Candra, Lakṣmī, and alike, as well as a śivali ṅga are also established in the hiti premises. The Kathmandu Municipality has placed a huge plastic water tank to the northwest of the hiti . The municipality fills it with tankers to distribute water to local residents when the hiti runs dry during the non-monsoon seasons. There are no evidences to date the initial construction of the hiti . The iconographic program of the statues found in the hiti premises suggest that it could have been built sometime during the Late Malla Period (1482-1768 CE). The stone inscription (from NS 942, i.e. 1822) installed in the hiti premises records the a pavement of the road above the hiti and of the yard in front of the Bhīmasena Mandira. Bhindyaḥ is one of the few hitis still in use. It is a major source of water for the local community during the monsoon. Unfortunately, it merely drips during the non-monsoon seasons. Local residents believe that washing clothes and taking shower in the hiti in the past contributed to its sullied appearance. Thus, a local club, Youth for Nation Nepal, has prohibited to wash clothes and take shower in the present-day. Water from this hiti is compulsorily used for the daily rituals in the nearby Bhīmsena Mandira. Once in every twelve years, the Bhīmsena statue is shifted to a secret room inside the temple. At the end of this ritual, this statue is ritually cleaned with the water collected from the hiti . The above-mentioned club and the local community periodically clean the hiti premises." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/KAT0930> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/KAT0930> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KAT0950> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Kapileśvara Mahādeva Mandira (built in 1690 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KAT0950"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Kapileśvara Mahādeva Mandira is located adjacent to the Bicche Bāhāḥ, Kathmandu. It faces west. The current stone pinnacle of the temple was installed after the previous golden one was stolen. A stone tora ṇ a featuring chepu adorns the entrance of the sanctum. A Śivaliṅga is consecrated in the sanctum, and there is also an additional Śivaliṅga inside the sanctum area, its jalaharī faces east. The inscription installed to the north of the sanctum entrance informs us that it was constructed by Kaṃpālidāsa (Kapiladāsa) and his wife Māneśvarī in 1690 CE (NS 810). The inscription does not mention the caste of Kaṃpālidāsa, but local residents believe that he was from a Pradhāna caste. His family also constructed the Bicche Phalcā ( KAT0941 ) which is adjacent to the temple. Kaṃpilādāsa&rsquo;s family endowed land to the temple and established a gu ṭ hī to finance the daily, annual, occasional, and other rituals. However, no official ritual activities take place in the temple in the present-day. Local community members visit the temple and worship the Śivaliṅga in early mornings. A fire oblation is performed on the day of Śrīpa&ntilde;camī (Sarasvatīpūjā) in the adjacent Bicche Phalcā ( KAT0941 ). Its gu ṭ hī members also perform a special worship in this temple on that day. Reṇukā Vajrācārya, a member of the local community, has been voluntarily cleaning the temple every Tuesday." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/KAT0950> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/KAT0950> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KAT1000> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Cibhāḥkhyaḥ Caitya" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KAT1000"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Cibhāḥkhyaḥ Caitya is located at Kaṅkeśvarī, Kathmandu. It is constructed on a relatively open space and this city quarter is named Cibhāḥkhyaḥ after the caitya . It has a three-level plinth, constructed with bricks, and plastered with bajra mortar. There is a staircase on the western fa&ccedil;ade of the caitya . Statues of four Buddhas are placed in its four cardinal directions. Dissimilar to many other caityas , the shape of Chibhāḥkhyaḥ Caitya&rsquo;s dome ( garbha ) is unique (see the monument photos). Motifs of four Buddhas are kept on the hali ṃ pau above the harmikā. The local community members believe that this is one of the Aśoka Caityas and was constructed by King Aśoka himself. It is believed that the place, where the caitya stands, was a pond of milk ( durupukhū ), Aśoka filled it, and constructed the caitya . However, there is no evidence to verify these beliefs. Pruscha (1975, vol. II, p. 38) mentions that a person called Kelayel Tuladhar constructed this caitya . However, neither there is evdience to prove this claim, nor the local elders are aware of it. Pruscha might have misspelled the above-mentioned name. Some of the local elders believe that the Śreṣṭhā family who runs the Hotel Ambassador at the Lazimpat Road, Kathmandu, constructed Chibhākhyaḥ Caitya. The caitya was renovated in 1983 CE (VS 2040) after a tree, sprouting out of the caitya &rsquo;s upper part, t rayodaśabhuvana , threatened to topple it. In the present-day, no one worships this caitya . Previously, the above-mentioned Śreṣṭhā family members used to worship it on the full moon day of Vaiśākha. A local community member, named Radhākṛṣṇa Śāhī, takes care of the caitya in the present-day." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/KAT1000> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/KAT1000> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KAT1051> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Yatkhā Bāhāḥ Caitya" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KAT1051"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "There are only few examples of big caityas constructed in the center of a bāhāḥ courtyard. Its shape closely resembles a Vajradhātu Caitya found in abundance in the Valley. Statues of Akṣobhya, Ratnasambhava, Amitābha, and Amoghasiddhi are located in niches facing east, west, south, and north respectively. The caitya &rsquo;s harmika , toraṇa , trayodaśabhuvana , and its pinnacle are covered with copper gilt. A ya ṣṭ hī is installed inside it. It is one of the oldest caityas in the Valley with its old ya ṣṭ hī remaining intact ( Gutschow 1997, 24). Locals believe that there is an Aśoka Caitya inside the caitya &rsquo;s garbha ( H. Śākya NS 1099, 31). There is scant inscriptive evidence to appoint caitya &rsquo;s initial construction. H. Śākya writes that the caitya &rsquo;s current look dates back to the nineteenth century. In 1868 CE (NS 989), an individual named Cikidhana Tāmrākāra renovated the caitya, and offered a gilt cakrāvalī and parasol at the top of the caitya . During this renovation, the caitya &rsquo;s ya ṣṭ hī was also replaced. A statue of Lokeśvara carved from the original ya ṣṭ hī is kept safe inside the caitya &rsquo;s garbha ( H. Śākya NS 1099, 46-46) . It was also renovated in 1976 CE (VS 2035). In the present-day (in 2021 CE), the Federal Government is renovating residential buildings, the dyaḥchẽ , and caitya &rsquo;s plinth. A local Udāy Guṭhī organises the anniversary of the caitya on the full moon day of Asauja (Āśvina). Tutaḥ Guṭhī members recite strotras in front of the caitya during the entire month of Guṃlā as well as on other auspicious days." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/KAT1051> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/KAT1051> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KAT1210> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Baku Nani" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KAT1210"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Baku Nani is one of the courtyards within the Itum Bāhāḥ Ṭola. It is located to the south of Itum Bāhāḥ ( KAT1220 ). Local community members believe that it is named after the fact that the Nani is located at the lower part of the extended Itum Bāhāḥ courtyard. There is a shrine in the middle of the Nani. A caitya is consecrated inside it, which is the only heritage property in the Nani. Similar caitya examples are also present elsewhere in front of the Itum Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ, in Kvāḥ Bāhāḥ, and in Kāyegu Nani. There is no evidence to appoint the initial construction of this caitya . In an inscription inside the sanctum from 1853 CE (NS 973), it is mentioned that the caitya was renovated. In the inscription, the caitya is referred to as Vajradhātu Caitya. Both Pruscha (1975, 44) and Locke (1985, 290) mention that the temple was constructed in a traditional way and tiled with jhĩgaṭī bricks. However, the temple's entire structure transformed into a cemented construction when it was renovated in 1987 CE. A Śākya family residing in the nani takes care of the temple and performs daily worship. Previously, on certain days every month, devotees gathered around the temple to recite mantras and other religious texts. This tradition takes place in the present-day twice a year on the full moon day of Kārttika and during the Kathmandu Pa&ntilde;cadāna Festival. On the full moon day of Kārttika, the local residents display various images made of fried pulses and grains. This tradition also continues to this day." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/KAT1210> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/KAT1210> .

