<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KAT2637> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Siddhimukteśvara Mandira (1855 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KAT2637"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Siddhimāna Rājabhaṇḍārī constructed the temple at the Pacalī Ghāṭa in the memory of his father Siddhinārāyaṇa Rājabhaṇḍārī in 1854 CE. Siddhimāna also constructed Lakṣmīnārāyaṇa ( KAT2636 ) and Dhaneśvara Mandira ( KAT2638 ). The temple was consecrated on the full moon day of the bright fortnight of Māgha in 1855 CE (VS 1911). A stone śivali ṅ ga, which was brought from India, is consecrated as the main deity inside its sanctum. Siddhimāna was one of the trusted elites of Jaṅga Bahādura Rāṇā and fiercely supported the Rāṇā regime. He was a subbā in 1846 CE, and in 1859 CE, he became a treasurer ( khajā&ntilde;cī ). He was appointed as the governor for Banke, Bardiya, Kailali, and Kanchanpur districts, collectively known as the Nayā Muluka &lsquo;New Kingdom&rsquo; in 1864 CE. Siddhimāna played a significant role in the economic upliftment of these districts ( Adhikari 1984, 219). He also accompanied Jaṅga Bahādura to his official state visit to the Great Britain in 1850 CE ( Rana 1974, 116). He had also established a gu ṭ hī and endowed land to sustain the daily worship in this temple. However, neither the endowed land nor the gu ṭhī exist in the present-day. Previously, a Brahmin priest was appointed as the priest to perform the daily rituals and he was paid a monthly salary of four thousand rupees. In the present-day, Viṣṇudevī Rājabhaṇḍārī, Siddhimāna Rājabhaṇḍārī&rsquo;s fifth-generation daughter-in-law, performs the daily worship in the temple. She also operates a home for elderly in the temple premises. The anniversary celebration of the temple takes place every year on the thirteenth day of the bright fortnight of Vaiśākha. On the day of Śivarātri, a big light ( mahādīpa ) is lit in the temple premises and a special worship is performed on the birthday of Kṛṣṇa (Kṛṣṇajanmāṣṭamī). A person called Rāmacandra Thāpā, who runs a paint shop in Ṭeku, Kathmandu, has been painting and repairing the temple from time to time." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/KAT2637> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/KAT2637> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR0002> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Cilancva Bāhāḥ (ca. 1515 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR0002"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "The Cilancva area is the highest point in Kirtipur, located in its eastern part. A huge Cilancva Caitya ( KIR0001 ) stands at the centre of the site. There are numerous other Buddhist monuments in the Caitya premises, which is collectively known as Cilancva Bāhāḥ, a major Buddhist monastery in Kirtipur. It is also called Jagatapāla Mahāvihāra, Mū Bāhāḥ, Gũ Vihāra, Padmakāṣṭhagīri Vihāra, and Kipū Bāhāḥ etc. Kirtipur was under the Patan rule during the medieval period. Thus, Cilancva Bāhāḥ is listed as one of the Mahāvihāras of Patan. The Nepālikabhūpavaṃśāvalī (19.3.26) also mentions that King Siddhinarasiṃha Malla listed this Bāhāḥ as one of the fifteen Mahāvihāras maintained by Patan. The members of this Bāhāḥ still part take in the Buddhist rituals and festivals held in Patan such as the Samyak Festival and other seasonal processions. Majority of the scholars conclude that this Bāhāḥ was constructed by Jagata Varmā, a Mahāpātra of Patan, almost at the same time when he constructed Cilancva Caitya ( KIR0001 ) in 1515 CE. However, the inscription recording the construction of this Caitya does not explicitly mention Cilancva Bāhāḥ, but refers to this area as 'Padmakāṣṭhagīri which resembles the heaven ( padmakāṣṭhagīri bhanne nāmale svargajastai suhāiraheko ṭhāũ ). It indicates that Jagata Pāla could have established a Bāhāḥ called Padmakāṣṭhagīri in the Cilancva premises which was later known as Cilancva Bāhāḥ. [1] He also renovated Bāghabhairava Mandira ( KIR0004 ) in 1571 CE (NS 635). Since his wife Rūpalakṣmī (cf. D. Vajrācārya VS 2056, 478) was from Kirtipur ( Locke 1985, 180), she could have encouraged him to construct and renovate these highly significant monuments. The southern entrance to Cilancva Caitya and the main entrance to Cilancva Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s dyaḥchẽ are parallel to each other, revealing the architectural similarity of both monuments. A pair of lions installed in 1716 CE (NS 836) flanks the dyaḥchẽ &rsquo;s main entrance. Similarly, two metal statues (Śāriputra and Maudgalyāyana) are placed to the right and to the left of the entrance respectively, which is a common feature of the bāhāḥs . A carved wooden toraṇa also crowns the entrance. Right above the toraṇa , a pasukājhyāḥ can be seen on the dyaḥchẽ&rsquo;s first floor. In addition to the main entrance, the dyaḥchẽ has two additional doors in its fa&ccedil;ade. Two trikhājhyāḥs are also installed on the dyaḥchẽ&rsquo;s first floor, right above these additional doors. The wooden toraṇa above the main entrance was stolen sometime ago, thus the toraṇa , crowning the eastern door, was shifted here. The toraṇas , kept above its eastern door, were removed during the renovation of the dyaḥchẽ building in 2015 CE. A statue of Cakrasamvara is concentrated as the āgaṃdyaḥ on the dyaḥchẽ&rsquo;s first floor. Until a few decades ago, one could see some traditional wall paintings on the first floor. They were completely destroyed during the renovation of the building (cf. Ś . Śreṣṭha VS 2057, 67). The first floor is now empty and used by the members of the saṅgha for organising the community feasts, lectures, seminars etc. There is a balcony on the fa&ccedil;ade of this floor. The roof of the dyaḥchẽ is tiled with jhĩgaṭī and a pura crowned by three pinnacles is installed on its top. Locke (1985, 180) mentions the beams, which support the aforementioned balcony, bearing the inscriptions from 1629 CE (NS 749) and 1680 CE (NS 800). They suggest that the Bāhāḥ must have been renovated in those dates. A local resident named Candramāna Vajrācārya repaired the roof in around 1889 CE after it was destroyed by a wind ( Ś . Śreṣṭha VS 2057, 68). The latest renovation of the dyaḥchẽ was carried out by the Department of Archaeology after it was destroyed during the 2015 CE earthquakes. Cilancva Bāhāḥ has a saṅgha consisting of the members from the Śākya and Vajrācārya clans. Each Vajrācārya member is responsible to carry out the daily worship for two weeks. The ritual worship is followed by a traditional music, gambāḥsĩ , which is a unique feature of the major bāhāḥs of Patan. The anniversary worship of the Bāhāḥ is held on the full moon day of Asoja. The statue of Cakrasamvara, concerted inside the āgaṃ, is worshiped by the Pradhāna caste community members of Kirtipur on the dark moon day of Vaiśākha (the Mātātīrtha Aũśī day) as their clan deity. The Bāhāḥ had a guṭhī consisting of six ropānīs of land for each elder member ( thakālī ) of the saṅgha for performing the daily and annual rituals. In addition to this, the guṭhī had a pond and flower garden. However, the mentioned guṭhī property cannot be traced out in present-day. The Bāhāḥ had a huge collection of metal statues dating from the seventh and eighth centuries. Because of a dispute that arose amongst the saṅgha members in around 1980 CE, the statues were handed over to the Guṭhī Saṃsthāna and National Museum (cf. Ś . Śreṣṭha VS 2057, 68-69). Footnotes ^ This Bāhāḥ is referred to as &lsquo;Padmakāṣṭhagīri Gũ Vihāra concerted according to the customs&rsquo; in the invitation letter sent from Paśupati Bāhāḥ to the member of Cilancva Bāhāḥ in 1574 (NS 694) requesting them to participate to the Samyak festival (cf. Vajrācārya VS 2061, 117). Since the invitation letter does not refer to the Bāhāḥ as consecrated by Jagatapāla, one may cast doubt on whether he was its initial constructor." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/KIR0002> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/KIR0002> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR0017> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Majāḥdegaḥ (built sometime between seventeenth and eighteenth century)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR0017"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Majāḥdegaḥ is a caitya standing on a steep slope to the south of the Cilancva Bāhāḥ ( KIR0001 ). It is built atop four-layered stone plinth. Unlike many other caityas in Kirtipur, the plinths of Majāḥdegaḥ are larger in size. Its topmost plinth is entirely made of stones. The local residents believe that these plinths must have been raised to build a temple,however no temple was ever built. For this reason, they refer to it as Majāḥdegaḥ, meaning incomplete temple . However, there is no evidence to verify these assumptions. Images of Tārā and Buddha are carved on four cardinal directions of the caitya , in accordance with the traditions. Besides these images, some smaller icons of other deities (similar to the ones carved on Yatālibi Caitya ( LAL2250 ) and found in Micchu Bāhāḥ ( LAL1100 )) are also carved on it (cf. Ś. Śre ṣṭ ha VS 2057, 64). Above these statues, a dome, harmikā, thirteen bhuvanas, and āmalaka motifs are carved. There is no evidence to date the initial construction of this monument. However, the motifs carved on the caitya suggest that it was perhaps built sometime between seventeenth and eighteenth century. It is probable that someone from the Vajrācārya castes living around this caitya might have constructed it. In 1990s, an iron sheet was welded around this caitya for its protection ( Shokoohy and others 2014, 313). The Kirtipur Municipality renovated its plinths in 2010 CE. There is no gu ṭ hī associated to this caitya . Even the elders of the area cannot recall any religious, social, or cultural activity (other than Bāhāḥ Cāḥ Hilegu) that used to be organised here." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/KIR0017> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/KIR0017> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR0018> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Kusi Bāhāḥ (ca. 17th-18th Cen.)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR0018"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Kusi Bāhāḥ is one of the branches ( kacā ) of the Cilancva Bāhāḥ ( KIR0002 ), located at Kusicā, to the southwest of the Cilancva Bāhāḥ premises. Kusi Bāhāḥ is also known by its Sanskrit name, Mahendrakīrti Vihāra, and (cf. Ś. Śre ṣṭ ha VS 2057, 69). Locke (1985, 182) mention it as Mahākīrti Vihāra. The bāhāḥ is accessible after walking down a flight of fifteen staircases through the ground floor of a private residential building. From the outside of this building, it is hard to assume that there is a bāhāḥ behind it. This could be the reason why Karl Pruscha was not able to locate and include this bāhāḥ in his inventory (published in 1975 CE). The bāhāḥ has a rectangular courtyard and its dya ḥchẽ (main shrine) faces northeast. The door of the dya ḥ chẽ is intricately carved, however, it lacks a tora ṇ a . Instead of a tora ṇ a , an image of Vajrasattva is carved on the door&rsquo;s upper part. A stone statue of Akṣobhya is consecrated as the kvāpādya ḥ ( bāhāḥ&rsquo; s primary deity) inside the dyaḥchẽ&rsquo;s sanctum . There used to be a mural on the wall inside the sanctum, however, it is completely destroyed. The dya ḥchẽ has a pasukājhyā ḥ (traditional wooden window) and an āgaṃ (shrine for the bāhāḥ &rsquo;s esoteric deity) on its first floor. Cakrasaṃvara is consecrated in the āga ṃ (based on a personal conversation with a local resident, from a Vajrācārya caste, male, age 56). Originally, the roof of the dya ḥchẽ was tiled with jhĩga ṭ ī. After it started leaking, the responsible Vajrācārya clan replaced it with corrugated pewter sheets in 2001 CE. A Śikharakūṭa Caitya is established in the middle of the bāhāḥ courtyard. There is no evidence to date the bāhāḥ &rsquo;s initial construction. The wood carving on the dya ḥ chẽ can be dated from the seventeenth and eighteenth century. A Vajrācārya family, residing in front of the bāhāḥ, are members of the saṅgha of the Cilancva Bāhāḥ, and responsible for maintaining the Bāhāḥ and carrying out the daily and other rituals. They believe that one of their ancestors constructed it. Two Śākya families, who are not members of the sa ṅgha of the Cilancva Bāhāḥ, perform their initiation ( bare chuigu ) ceremonies here. These families moved to Kirtipur some generations back and the Vajrācāryas of the Cilancva Bāhāḥ gave them the permission to use Kusi Bāhāḥ for their initiation ceremonies (cf. Locke 1985, 182). Every year, on the full moon day of the bright fortnight of Śrāvaṇa, the main gate of the bāhāḥ is open for twenty-four hours to allow the local community members to worship the kvāpādya ḥ . On other days, the main gate and the sanctum entrance are kept locked." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/KIR0018> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/KIR0018> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR0020> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Yākaḥ Bāhāḥ (built in 1647 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR0020"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Yāka Bāhāḥ is located on the northwestern part of the Cilancva Caitya premises ( KIR0001 ) and is one of the branches ( kacā ) of Cilancva Mahāvihāra ( KIR0002) . The Bāhāḥ is also called Jīvadharma Mahāvihāra (see the inscription from NS 801 below), and its courtyard can be entered through an astonishingly carved wooden door. A set of artistically carved trikhā windows can be seen above this door. The Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s dyaḥchẽ (house for the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s primary deity) is built in the courtyard, facing east. A stone statue of Akṣobhya is enshrined as the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ (primary deity) inside the sanctum, which is located on the dayaḥchẽ&rsquo;s ground floor. Besides the kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ , there were several other small Buddhist statues and caityas inside the sanctum, however, they were stolen in the course of time. A pair of lions and elephants (established in 1710 CE, i.e., 830 NS) are installed on either side of the entrance to the sanctum. The entrance is adorned with traditional carvings but has no toraṇa above it. The dyaḥchẽ&rsquo;s first floor has a set of pasukā windows, and its roof is covered with corrugated pewter sheets ( jastāpātā ). The inscription dating 1680 CE (801 NS) installed in the Bāhāḥ premises attests that Yākaḥ Bāhāḥ was constructed in 1647 CE (767 NS) by the Rathasyana Vajrācārya, his son Jīvarāja, and other family members. It also mentions that King Siddhinarasiṃha Malla, too, was present during the inaugural ceremony of the Bāhāḥ. Jīvarāja, the main founder of the Bāhāḥ, died sixteen years after its completion. Several statues of Buddha, as well as, Lokeśvara, Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā, Gaṇeśa, and Mahākāla statues were installed in the Bāhāḥ premises in the memory of Jivarāja by his family members. On the same date, his two sons also endowed four and a half ropanīs of land and established a guṭhī to sponsor the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s anniversary rituals. The other inscriptions (see below) installed in the Bāhāḥ suggest that a number of statues and architectural artifacts were installed here in the late medieval period. In 1970s, the statue of Cakrasamvara and other metal statues enshrined inside the āgaṃ (the shrine on the dyaḥchẽ&rsquo;s first floor built for the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s esoteric deities and rituals), were also stolen. Due to the lack of regular maintenance, the roof of the dyaḥchẽ collapsed in 2009 CE, and covered the kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ . In 2011CE, some residents of the region restored the roof, covering it with corrugated pewter sheets ( jastāpātā ) funded by the local government. The building which has the main entrance to the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s courtyard was damaged during the 2015 CE earthquakes. Due to a dispute over its ownership, no one has renovated it since then. Although the inscription (see below) offers a detailed account of the traditions of daily worship, annual rituals, lighting up oil lamp on the full moon day of Kārtika, and so forth to be performed in the Bāhāḥ, no one carries out these rituals in the present-day. Until two decades ago, a Vajrācārya caste member used to perform the daily worship and annual rituals in the Bāhāḥ. Unfortunately, no one took over these tasks after he died. There is no one left who is directly related to this Bāhāḥ, thus it is slowly falling into oblivion." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/KIR0020> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/KIR0020> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR0062> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Bhavānīśaṅkara Mandir (built in 1718 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR0062"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This śikhara -styled Bhavānīśaṅkara Mandira is located in the Bāghabhairava Temple complex ( KIR0004 ). It has four doors, each crowned with a stone t oraṇa , however, the sanctum is only accessible through the one which faces west. The remaining three which face east, north, and south, are false doors. [1] The temple is stone-built and stands on a two-layered plinth. The bell-shaped roof of the temple is capped by a jvālā nhyākaṃ , functioning as its pinnacle. The present stone statue of Bhavānīśaṅkara, consecrated inside the sanctum, was installed in 1984 CE after the old one was stolen (cf. Ś. Śre ṣṭ ha VS 2057,43). An inscription records that Siddhirāma Bhāro and his wife Gaṃgāmayi, residents of Kirtipur, built this temple and consecrated a stone statue of Bhavānīśaṅkara inside its sanctum in 1718 CE (NS 838). A statue of this couple is installed in front of the temple. The inscription further mentions that they also established a guṭhī having endowed five ropanīs of land in order to maintain the temple and carry out the daily rituals. However, there is no information about the guṭhī and the land. Even the local community elders are not aware of any guṭhī or land related to this temple. The priest of Bāghabhairava Temple performs the daily rituals. The Department of Archaeology cleaned and repaired the Bāghabhairava Temple complex in around 2007 CE including the Bhavānīśaṅkara Mandira. Footnotes ^ The door in the north fa&ccedil;ade has an inscription recording the initial construction of the Temple. The second inscription found inside the sanctum is not related to this Temple." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/KIR0062> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/KIR0062> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR0065> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Nārāyaṇa Mandira (ca. 1803 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR0065"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "The Nārāyaṇa Mandira (Temple) is located inside the Bāghabhairava Temple Complex ( KIR0004 ) in Kirtipur. The door to its sanctum which is ornately designed and carved, rests on a stone pillar base ( īlvaha ṃ ) and is flanked by a pair of stone lions. Generally, such a type of door architecture can be seen only in tiered temples. Thus, we can assume that the Nārāyaṇa Mandira too was originally a tiered-styled temple. There are no historical sources to date the initial construction of this Temple. However, Ś. Śre ṣṭ ha (VS 2057, 45), without refereeing to any sources, mentions that it was constructed by Hastikā (one of the nannies of King Raṇabahādura Śāha) in 1803 CE during the renovation of the Bāghabhairava Temple ( KIR0004 ). The Temple&rsquo;s pinnacle is octagonal and is gilded. According to the elders of this area, the octagonal gilded pinnacle was established by a certain Jītavīra (who was supposedly a government employee), a resident of the Tuṃjhvaḥ Ṭola (Kirtipur) when he renovated the Temple in 1932 CE (VS 1988) by the order of Prime Minister Juddha Śamśera (r. 1 September 1932 - 29 November 1945). The inscription inscribed on the pinnacle records this information. It was probably during this renovation the temple&rsquo;s structure was changed from a tiered style to a domed style. Such a domed style temple architecture was popular during the time of Prime Minister Jaṅga Bahādura Rāṇā. From his time onwards, several damaged temples were reconstructed in the domed shape. The present look of the Nārāyaṇa Mandira dates back to 1952 CE (VS 2009). Around the same time, a gu ṭ hī was established to look after the Temple and conduct the daily rituals here ( Ś. Ś ṛ e ṣṭ ha , 1994, 87). A statue of Śrīdharaviṣṇu standing on the samapāda posture is consecrated inside its sanctum. The statue has four arms: he is carrying a cakra (wheel) on his upper right hand and a gadā (mace) on his upper left hand. Similarly, a padma (lotus) is on his lower right hand and a śan ̇ kha (conch shell) is on his lower left hand. The Śrīdharavisṇu statue is flanked by a Sarasvatī (left) and Lakṣmī (right) statue. These two female deities are believed to be Viṣṇu&rsquo;s wives in the Kathmandu Valley from the medieval time ( R. Vanta VS 2061, 19). Beside these, a statue of a donor couple in greeting posture ( namaskāramudrā ) is also erected inside the sanctum. The descendants of Jītavīra who had renovated the Temple in 1932 CE used to clean the Temple premises, paint the Temple and perform its anniversary rituals. They had also endowed some plots of land for this purpose. However, this tradition came to an end sometime during the decade of 2000s CE (local knowledge, local resident, Maharjana, male, age 69)." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/KIR0065> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/KIR0065> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR0066> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Mahādyaḥ Degaḥ (built around seventeenth and eighteenth century)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR0066"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Mahādyaḥ Degaḥ is one of the numerous small temples built in the Bāghabhairava Temple Complex ( KIR0004 ). A Śivaliṅga is consecrated in the sanctum of this Degaḥ, and there is also one other Śivaliṅga in the sanctum. There is no evidence to date the initial construction of the temple. However, a careful study of its wooden elements suggests that the temple might have been built around seventeenth and eighteenth century. A sketch of the temple drawn by Henry Ambrose Oldfield around 1855 CE shows it in ruins with both roofs damaged completely. It is probable that the temple suffered severe damages during the 1832 CE earthquakes and was not renovated till 1850s. In the same sketch, a tora ṇ a can be seen crowning the temples's western door, which it does not exist in the present-day. In the Rāṇā Period, the temple was reconstructed with a domed roof. It was dismantled and reconstructed as a tiered temple again in 1999 CE (VS 2056) (local knowledge, a local resident, Maharjana, male, age 53). An inscription from 1844 CE (NS 964) is installed on the southern wall of the temple. It mentions that a phalcā was constructed around the place of Yamaleśvara, and reminds the members of the gu ṭhī about their obligations and duties. The phalcā is not related to the temple. On the basis of this inscription, Ś. Śre ṣṭ ha (VS 2057, 44) named this temple as Yamaleśvara Mandira. There is no guthi associated with Mahādyaḥ Degaḥ. Even elders of the local community cannot recall any gu ṭhī or any social or religious activities associated with the temple. A group of local youth lights up a bonfire on the day of Śivarātrī in front of the temple." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/KIR0066> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/KIR0066> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR4066> a hg:DhungeDhara ;
    rdfs:label "Kvāḥcva Khyālidyaḥ Phalcā" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/KIR4066"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This non-existing rest house was constructed by a person named Dharaṇīdhara in 1694 CE (NS 814). Only a small shrine of Lakṣmīnārāyaṇa remains at the center of the square pavilion where the Phalcā once stood. The statue of Lakṣmīnārāyaṇa faces west towards Umāmaheśvara Mandira ( KIR0006 ) while the inscription recording the construction of the Phalcā is set on the rear wall of the shrine. The inscription states that Dharaṇīdhara, who constructed the Phalcā in 1694 CE, was a resident of Kirtipur and held the post of the chief of the fort. The fort probably is a reference to the city of Kirtipur. It further mentions that the Phalcā&rsquo;s architecture was similar to Kaṣṭhamaṇḍapa of Kathmandu. The donor also endowed seven ropanī s of land to form a guthi for performing various rituals throughout the year ( Shokoohy and others 2014, p. 94). The Phalcā is due to be reconstructed in traditional architecture with funds from the US embassy. The statue of Lakṣmīnārāyaṇa exudes the emotion of love, as the four-armed Nārāyaṇa is seen in amorous disposition, embracing his consort Lakṣmī while slightly bending towards her. Locals, therefore, call the deity &lsquo;Khyālidyaḥ&rsquo;, the deity of love. There is a local belief that worshipping Khyālidyaḥ brings the person&rsquo;s love affair to a successful conclusion. The inscription also instructs concerned persons to organize the hāthu hāyke tradition. This proves that the tradition of discharging fermented beer ( thvã ) from the Bhairava mask (Hāthudyaḥ) existed here. This tradition is no longer in practice." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/KIR4066> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/KIR4066> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0080> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "I Bāhāḥ (16th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0080"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "I Bāhāḥ is located approximately 50 metres south of Ikhālakhu Square on the way to Tabāhāl, and lies on the right-hand side of the street. The Bāhāḥ can be accessed through an opening on a boundary wall to the east. It has been heavily reconstructed and was converted to a four-storey residential building after the 2015 earthquakes. The original architecture of the building has been completely destroyed, so that only traces of the brass-clad door, two blind windows and some layers of cornices remain today (in 2019). The main building is raised on a plinth which is 60 cm high. At the centre of the stone-paved courtyard one can find a vajradhātu caitya and a dharmādhātu maṇḍala . The courtyard is surrounded by residential buildings on three of the four cardinal directions, with the eastern side as the exception. The courtyard has two alleyways that lead out to the smaller courtyards; these can be found to its west and its south. The Bāhāḥ is a private vihāra constructed by a Joshi family; it is not a branch of any larger bāhāḥ . Images of lions sitting on elephants can be found on either side of the main entrance, and small carved windows adorn the wall here. Upon entering the sanctum, one immediately sees an image of Akṣobhya facing to the east. Two brass statues of Yama holding kāladaṇḍa (the staff of death) and pāśa (a noose) are also housed in the sanctum, with one on either side of the main door. An empty triangular niche to the left of the door is worshipped as Hanumān. Accoding to Pruscha (1975, 133), this vihāra was constructed in the 16th century. According to Locke (1985, 140) two metal flags used to flank the door and a toraṇa was installed above the main entrance. This contained an image of Akṣobhya. However, it seems that the toraṇa was removed by the residing family of the vihāra some time around 2008. Moreover, Locke states that a trikhā jhyāla (triple window) and a bāku jhyāla (lit. halved window) can be found in the fa&ccedil;ade of the first floor. The building was severely damaged and was no longer safe to live in. Therefore the owner demolished the building and reconstructed it in a modern style. After the reconstruction the family sold the building. Local people say that the family also sold the ground floor of the building where the kvāpādyaḥ of the vihāra is enshrined. The bell was donated in VS 1959." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0080> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0080> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0081> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Ikhālakhu Bāhāḥ" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0081"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This small courtyard, known as Ikhālakhu Bāhāḥ, is located to the east of Ikhālakhu coka . The courtyard is behind a row of residential buildings near the Ikhālakhu Jahru . It is paved with stone flooring. This small courtyard is located to the east of Ikhālakhu and can be reached through a narrow alley. Measuring 6.5m x 3.75m, it consists of a raised plinth on the northern wall containing three small statues with stone roofs, atop of which are small stupas. At the center is a śikharakūṭa caitya with a base measuring 140 x 140 cm and a height of 170 cm. A d harmadhātu maṇḍala (90 cm tall) adjoins it on its northern side. A small caitya (70cm tall) can be found on the southern plinth attached to the building, and appears to be in a neglected state. A bell hangs on two stone pillars (13 x 13 cm and 170 cm high) to the east of the courtyard. This tiny courtyard reaches the minimum requirements for a Bāhāḥ as defined by Locke. Bruce Owens notes that the kwāḥpāḥ dyaḥ , caitya , and other features are under threat as this space has been used to store lumber (and now detritus from the reconstruction) since at least 2008. Indeed, most of the features generally found in a vihāra cannot be found here. There is, for instance, no god-house ( dyaḥchẽ ). Local people therefore consider this place to just be a coka rather than a vihāra . Three south-facing and connected shrines house the main deities of the vihāra. When Locke (1985, 140) studied this place, there was an image of Akṣobhya inside the middle shrine, an image of Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā inside the shrine right to it and an image of Ṣaḍakṣarī Lokeśvara inside the left shrine, however these images have since been stolen. In their place, statues of a doner in the act of praying have been installed inside the middle shrine, whilst the right-hand shirine now contains an image of Lakṣmī holding a lotus flower in her hand. The left-hand shrine has been furnished with an image of a two-handed Lokeśvara holding a lotus in his left hand and with his right hand in a varada gesture. Above the middle and the left shrine, one can find images of Mahākāla and three doners. These have been placed in a disorganized way. Local people claim that this Bāhāḥwas made by Śreṣṭhas. Apart from this, nothing more is known about its history. Inside the courtyard, there is one śikharakūṭa caitya and a dharmadhātu maṇḍala . At the eastern corner of the courtyard is a bell that was donated in NS 1049, and at the southern corner one can find another small caitya . Note that the illustration identified by Locke (1985, 141I) as Ikhalakhu Baha is actually Iku Baha, and the photograph of Ikhalaku (mis-labeled as Iku Baha) is actually on p. 139." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0081> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0081> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0130> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Iku Bāhāḥ" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0130"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Iku Bāhāḥ, a very small, private bāhāḥ, is located in Ikhālakhu Ṭola, about 35m northeast of the Ikhālakhu square. Since there is no entrance through any of the streets, one must enter through the door of a private building situated towards the west of the monument. At the centre of the courtyard, there is a small shrine facing east with just a small opening that is 55cm wide and raised on a one meter-high platform. Inside the shrine is a tiny image of Akṣobhya Buddha. One might notice the unusual proportions of the statue of the Buddha and the large lotus on which he is sitting. A small caitya, made from stone and with images of Tathāgatas facing in all four cardinal directions, forms the pinnacle of the shrine. In front of the shrine, there is a dharmadhātu caitya in octagonal shape. There are images of eight Lokeśvaras on the dharmadhātu : Vajrapāṇi, Gaganaga&ntilde;ja, Ratnapāṇi, Kṣitigarbha, Padmapāṇi, Viśvapāṇi, Ratnagarbha and Sarvanivaraṇaviṣkambhin. On the northern wall of the residential building, one can find a niche containing an aniconic stone statue that is worshiped as Gaṇeśa. At the west wall of the āgãchẽ is a khaḍga . The āgãchẽ of the Bāhāḥ is to the northernmost end of the courtyard, and it is possible to exit via the northeastern corner. Pruscha writes, &ldquo;all that has remained of this Bahal is a small courtyard with plastered shrine and dharmadhatu in the form of a double petalled lotus. The shrine contains an image of Bhumisparsa Shakyamuni. It was constructed by Bekharatna Silakar in 1934 and its chief priest is a Bajracharya.&rdquo; ( Pruscha 2015: 207) The illustration identified as Ikhālākhu Bāhāḥ on p. 141 in Locke 1985 is actually Iku Bāhāḥ, and the photograph of Ikhālākhu (mislabeled as Iku Bāhāḥ) is in fact on p. 139." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0130> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0130> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0260> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Jena Bāhāḥ (ca. 15th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0260"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Jena Bāhāḥ, also known as Śrī J&ntilde;āna Nāma Vihāra, is located on the southern side of Jena Bāhāḥ Ṭola in front of the Gaṇeśa Mandira ( LAL0270 ). The courtyard can be accessed by passing through a metal door. The main structure of the bāhāḥ has mostly collapsed. Its foundations as well as wooden decorative elements are scattered around the site. The main structure on the left side of the metal door has been destroyed and is fenced off with CGI sheets. The square courtyard has a teliya tiled floor and contains a Licchavi caitya , a dharmadhātu maṇḍala at the southeastern corner and a Kṛṣṇa shrine with a brick dome at the center. To the south, one can also find a Durgā shrine raised on a platform 90 cm in height. This bāhāḥ is a branch of Hakha Bāhāḥ ( LAL0390 ). The building lacks a dyaḥchẽ , something which is normally a major feature of such bāhāḥ. Local people say that it was probably destroyed during the 1934 earthquake and never reconstructed. Locke (1985, 152) assumes that the bāhāḥ was abandoned after the earthquake. Later, Pradhānas, non-Buddhist Newar families from Kathmandu, bought the surrounding lands and constructed houses upon it. These buildings now surround the bāhāḥ area. There is no inscription at the site through which one could date the monument. Locke (1985, 154) argues that this branch predates the transfer of Hakha Bāhāḥ from its original place to its present location. This move took place during the reign of Siddhi Narasiṃha Malla (1620-1661). The Licchavi caitya in the bāhāḥ's courtyard supports this hypothesis." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0260> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0260> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0360> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Vãlā Bāhāḥ (1921 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0360"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "V&atilde;lā Bāhāḥ, a branch of Bu Bāhāḥ ( LAL0440) , is located to the east of Pūrṇacandī Temple and to the north of the street that leads to Ikhālakhu. The bāhāḥ can be accessed by turning left down a narrow alleyway. It is called a bāhāḥ or vihāra even though it lacks a kvāḥpāḥchẽ . A small shrine of the bāhāḥ is attached to the southern wall of the courtyard. The wooden carved door of the sanctum is surmounted by a toraṇa displaying an image of Vajrasattva. However, Locke (1985, 169) identifies the deity as Akṣobhya. Inside the sanctum, one can find a north-facing stone image of Akṣobhya. There are two small niches on either side of the shrine. According to Vajrācārya (1993, 134) there was once an image of Mahākāla in the western niche. However, this is now missing. The main temple structure is built on a double plinth. A stone threshold on the upper plinth provides the base for the carved wooden structure, which is open at three sides and has a rear boundary wall for support. The structure has two tiers of cement roof and a bell-shaped stūpa as its finial. Ten struts support the roof. There is a dharmadhātu jinālaya caitya in front of the main shrine. Hela Thakuṃ built this bāhāḥ in 1921 (NS 1041) in memory of her late son Harṣavīra Vajrācārya. He was the eldest son of Dhīrjavīra Kampanī, a famous devotee and donor living in Dhyākva Nhūchẽ Kavaḥ of Bu Bāhāḥ. A ceremony called v&atilde;lā was organised on the inauguration day of the bāhāḥ , during which a procession passed through the main bāhās and bahīs of Patan. This bāhāḥ is named after the procession ( Vajrācārya 1933, 134)." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0360> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0360> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0380> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Vachẽ Nani Bāhāḥcā (1909 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0380"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Vachẽ Nani Bāhāḥcā, a branch of Hakha Bāhāḥ ( LAL0390 ), is located behind the Haka Bāhāḥ and on the eastern side of a narrow street that goes from the Haka Bāhāḥ to Ikhālakhu. The bāhāḥcā is a small courtyard (7.2 m x 7.2 m) and can be accessed through another courtyard which is attached to the street. There is no kvaḥpāḥchẽ in this bāhāḥ . The main temple of the bāhāḥcā is built against the wall of a residential building facing west, and stands on a double plinth with a stone threshold. Timber lattice work surrounds the sanctum on three sides, whilst its rear wall is shared with the residential building. Inside, a statue of Akṣobhya has been placed on a high plinth so that it faces west. The roof is a two-tiered cement structure with a stone stūpa as its finial. A śikharakūṭotpannaparipadma caitya (187 cm high) can be found in front of the main temple structure at the centre of the courtyard. A statue of Gaṇeśa is located to the right of the temple and a statue of Hanumān can be found to its left. Both have been placed on the walls of the other buildings. This bāhāḥ was founded by Munirāja in 1909. The caitya was established prior to this in 1863, most likely by his father Dhanavantasiṃha Vajrācārya. This bāhāḥ is managed by three Vajrācārya families who are the descendants of Munirāja." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0380> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0380> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0420> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Bhailaḥsĩ Bāhāḥ (1896 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0420"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Bhailaḥsĩ Bāhāḥ, a branch of Bu Bāhāḥ ( LAL0440 ), can be reached via a narrow alley at the north-east corner of Bu Bāhāḥ and by passing through two small courtyards. This four-storey bāhāḥ stands in the north-west corner of its courtyard (10 m x 12 m). A statue of Akṣobhya facing east is enshrined in the main sanctum, which is located on the ground floor and can be reached by climbing the stairs of a three bayed phalcā with a front row of columns. The central bay of this structure is surmounted by a carved tora ṇa showing images of Akṣobhya, Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā and Lokeśvara. A painting of Buddhist images can be found on the mud plaster wall of the bāhāḥ's first floor. This level is also decorated with a lattice window flanked by two oval Tibetan-style paintings of Buddha&lsquo;s birth. A three-bayed window covers the principal fa&ccedil;ade on the third floor. An fourth floor has been added above the original floors, and now houses the āgama deity of the bāhā ḥ. A private house to the north of the bāhāḥ exhiits similar Tibetan-style images of Caturmahārāja painted on oval mud plaster. A dharmadhātu maṇḍala (85 cm high) is located in front of the bāhāḥ and a śikharakūṭa caitya (165 cm high) can be found at the center of the courtyard. There are three exits at the east, north-east, and south-east corners. This bāhāḥ was established in 1896 (NS 1016) and is named after its founder, Bhairavasiṃha Vajrācārya." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0420> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0420> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0451> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Devajyoti Bāhāḥ (1889 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0451"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Devajyoti Bāhāḥ is a branch of Bu Bāhāḥ ( LAL0440 ). It is located to the north of Bu Bāhāḥ and can be accessed via a narrow alley that leads from the monument's north-west corner. The main shrine building faces north and stands on a double plinth. It is attached to the rear boundary wall, which is decorated with a pair of pilaster columns on either side. The monument appears to have been constructed relatively recently and has an overhanging concrete roof with a bell-shaped pinnacle and a st ūpa above it. The shrine is plastered in lime and has been painted white. A stone statue of Akṣobhya is enshrined in the sanctum. Small statues of Gaṇeśa and Mahākāla have been attached to the boundary wall, on the left and right-hand sides of the shrine respectively. The courtyard (6.7 m x 4.2 m) is also home to a dharmadh ātu jin ālaya caitya (180 cm high) and a dharmadh ātu ma ṇḍala (90 cm high), both of which stand in front of the main monument and are lined up in its axis. The courtyard is surrounded by residential buildings and has a colonnaded structure on the ground floor. Two bells hang from the stone pillars on the eastern and western sides of the courtyard. This bāhāḥ was established by Devajyoti Vajrācārya in 1889. After his death, his wives established a guṭhī for the management of the bāhāḥ in 1897. The Mūladuvā clan (a priest clan) of Bu Bāhāḥ performs the daily worship in this shrine. Aside from this, all other social and religious activities related to this bāhāḥ have come to an end." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0451> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0451> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0460> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Devarāja Bāhāḥ (1902 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0460"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Devarāja Bāhāḥ is located in Pyagaḥ Thā̃ to the east of the street that runs from Gāḥ Bāhāḥ ( LAL0510 ) to Pim Bāhāḥ Caitya ( LAL0750 ). A somewhat hidden narrow door leads to the bāhāḥ&rsquo;s courtyard, where a small shrine of Akṣobhya is attached to a six-storey building. The shrine has a small door surmounted by a toraṇa showing an image of Akṣobhya and flanked by latticed windows on either side. The structure has a two-tiered lime plaster roof with a towering bell-shaped pinnacle and a small stone caitya . Carved struts support the roof. In the courtyard, one can find a dharmadhātujinālaya caitya (140 cm high). Paved with brick tiles, the sunken courtyard measures 235 cm &times; 275 cm. A stone inscription attached to the left wall of the shrine mentions the date of its initial construction. A member of Bu Bāhāḥ (LAL0440) called Devarāja Vajrācārya built this bāhāḥ in 1902, one year after he constructed the dharmadhātujinālaya caitya in the courtyard. It was worshipped daily and was a site for other religious activities to take place until the grandson of the founder, Dharmarāja Vajrācārya, passed away. The courtyard has since fallen into disuse and has become a dumping site for rubbish." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0460> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0460> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0470> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Iku Bāhāḥ (before 1878 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0470"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Iku Bāhāḥ is one of the branches of Bu Bāhāḥ ( LAL0440 ) and is located to the north-west of the main bāhāḥ . For this reason, this monastic building is also called ikuli or yakuli , meaning &lsquo;north-west direction&rsquo;. Nowadays, a family living in Mūladuvāḥ Kavaḥ (Nānicāyāḥ Kavaḥ) of Bu Bāhāḥ takes care of this branch. Residential buildings surround the three-storey monastic building. The main entrance is flanked by a lion to the right and a śārdūla (leopard) to the left. Both of these statues were established in 1885 CE. The entrance to the sanctum is clad in brass and is surmounted by a copper repouss&eacute; toraṇa showing Akṣobhya in the middle with Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā on his right and Lokeśvara on his left. An image of Padmapāṇi Bodhisattva has also been placed on the upper part of the toraṇa. The door to the shrine is flanked by small latticed windows, with a stone statue of Hanumān attached to the wall above the left window and an image of Gaṇeśa kept above the right window. On the left wall one can also find a stone statue of Mahākāla with three heads and four arms holding khaḍga , kartṛ , khaṭvāṅga and kapāla . The main deity ( kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ ) is Padmapāṇi Bodhisattva. The fa&ccedil;ade of the first storey contains a latticed window flanked by two inscriptions and two smaller latticed windows, whilst the second storey has a large plain window. Historical photos of the bāhāḥ show that it once had a jhĩgaṭī -tiled roof. However, the building is currently under reconstruction. A dharmadhātu maṇḍala and a śikharakūṭa caitya have been erected in the courtyard. In the 2015 earthquakes, a building standing to the right of the bāhāḥ collapsed and fell down, destroying two storeys of the bāhāḥ . This building was completely removed after the earthquake and is now under construction. The Lalitpur Municipality has provided 2.5 million rupees for its reconstruction. A photograph of the bāhāḥ complex taken circa 1974 (published in Pruscha 1975 C/P-47) shows that the ground floor of the bāhāḥ (as previously reconstructed) is similar to its current structure, with the exception that dāci apā (bricks) were used instead of mā apā (bricks) and the white plastered cornice was replaced with decorative bricks. The height of the first floor has been increased considerably (by approx. 50 cm) and the three old latticed windows were replaced with new ones of different sizes and proportions. A new carved brick cornice was added above them, matching the design of the ground floor cornice. The second floor now features a new wall made using dāci apā and a new window frame." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0470> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0470> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0480> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Sijaḥ Bāhāḥ (c. early 17th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0480"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This monastic compound is a branch of the larger monastery Bu Bāhāḥ ( LAL0440 ). It has a small shrine on its western side that faces east. This shrine takes the form of a one-storey temple based on a low stone plinth. The main deity has been placed on a raised plinth 90 cm above this base plinth. The plinth has a nāgvaḥ design (quarter round) profile at its base with a band of kasimvaḥ decorative bricks above it. A line of nāgabelī can be found on the brick wall around the middle of the plinth. The edge of the plinth consists of a black stone above a layer of lotus leaf bricks that have eroded due to weather exposure. On top of this plinth is a small carved timber structure which opens up at three sides and with a rear brick wall. The sanctum has a unique pitched roof; its three tiers are plastered in lime, and the upper part has a bell-shaped design. A stūpa forms the pinnacle of the temple. In front of the temple structure and at the center of the courtyard, one can find a vajradhātu caitya built on a square plan of 386 cm. A small dharmadhātu maṇḍala (80 cm high) has been built between the caitya and the temple structure. The square courtyard measures 13.3 m x 13.3 m and is surrounded by residential buildings in all directions, with a small open space at the south-east corner. Normally, one can see images of Mahākāla and Hanumān at the gate of most bāhāḥs, however this is not the case here. There is also no toraṇa above the main shrine's entrance. Locke (1985, 159) and Vajrācārya (1993, 117) both mention that the main deity in the sanctum used to be a statue of Amitābha. However, this has been replaced by a stone statue of an eastern-facing Akṣobhya. No inscription or other document providing any further information about the establishment of the Bāhāḥ could be uncovered. Although he cannot find hard evidence to prove his theory, Pruscha (2015, 140) argues that this monument was constructed in the 16th century. The Bāhāḥ was built by Śreṣṭhas, and Vajrācāryas from Bu Bāhāḥ used to perform the ritual worship in the shrine. The Śreṣṭha representatives bestowed lands upon the shrine to fund a guṭhī for its management ( Vajrācārya 1993, 117). Until the late 20th century, 5 pāthī of unhusked rice was collected every year for the Bāhāḥ from the harvest. Today, the situation has changed; the Bāhāḥ no longer receives income from the land and the daily ritual worship has come to an end." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0480> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0480> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0530> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Naḥ Bāhāḥcā (before 1548 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0530"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Naḥ Bāhāḥcā is one of the branches of Si Bāhāḥ ( LAL0690 ) and is located at the south-west corner of the largest courtyard of Naḥ Bāhāḥ Nani. One can reach the bāhāḥcā via a narrow underpass from the south-west corner of the nani . The two-storey building faces east, and an aṣṭakoṇoparipadma caitya stands in front of its sanctum. The principal fa&ccedil;ade of the monument building has a carved wooden door at its centre flanked by two small lattice windows. There is no toraṇa but small images of Akṣobhya, Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā and Lokeśvara surmounted by a small caitya image have been fixed to the lintel above the door. Two small niches made from wooden frames also flank the sanctum entrance. Stone statues of Gaṇeśa and Mahākāla have been placed in the niches to the left and right respectively. The front wall of the first storey has been separated into three sections. One part contains the sanctum door, one part has a Gaṇeśa statue and the third part holds a Bhairava statue. The joint line between these sections is clearly visible. A band of carved wooden cornice separates the first floor from the second floor. The fa&ccedil;ade of the first floor largely consists of an intricately carved three bay window ( pasukājhyāḥ ) which is flanked by two small blind windows. The remaining wall area has been painted white. Figures of Namasaṅgīti and Amoghapāśa are displayed on either side of the window, whilst images of the Seven Buddhas can be seen above the window. The roof of the god house is made from corrugated metal sheets and supported by plain struts. A stone statue of Akṣobhya is enshrined inside the sanctum. The literal meaning of Naḥ Bāhāḥ is &lsquo;a new monastic building&rsquo;. Recently, the monastic building located at the north-east corner of this small monastic building was known as Naḥ Bāhāḥ. However, locals claim that this Naḥ Bāhāḥcā (LAL0530) is the original Naḥ Bāhāḥ. In earlier times, a pond called Khāsāpukhū was located to the north west of this bāhāḥcā . However, the pond has since been filled in and a school named Svatantra Śikṣā Sadana runs this area. The bāhāḥcā was given its classical name, Yokulivarṇa Mahāvihāra, due to its proximity to the pond; yokuli means 'north-western direction' in the Nevārī language. No sources could be found with which to date the initial construction of this bāhāḥcā . Pruscha 1975 (1975, C/P-53) dates this monument to the 17th century and mentions that it was renovated by a guṭhī in 1933, though he does not provide evidence to support this claim. A palm-leaf manuscript dated NS 688 (1548 CE) makes mention of a monastic building named Naka Bāhāra. It could be that this refers to the present monument. Long ago, most of the saṅgha members of the bāhāḥcā were Śākyas who were carpenters by occupation. Locke (1985: 178) mentions that the last surviving male member of this household died without any sons. Afterwards, the husbands of his daughters inherited the rights, duties and properties of their father-in-law. Later, these families stopped performing social and religious activities in the bāhāḥcā . At present, four families affiliated with the Āagama Guṭhī of the Naḥ Bāhāḥ Nani perform daily ritual worships in the bāhāḥcā . When cuḍākaraṇa (shaving one's hair for the first time), initiations, jaṃko and other rituals are organised in the Si Bāhāḥ, these people also come to visit this bāhāḥcā ." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0530> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0530> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0700> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Dune Bāhāḥ" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0700"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Dune Bāhāḥ, a branch of Si Bāhāḥ ( LAL0690 ), is located to the west of the main Bāhāḥ and faces towards it also. Tall buildings surround the courtyard in which the building is located. The Bāhāḥ is also known as Bāhāḥcā, though its classical name is Duṇḍagṛha Bāhāḥ. Pruscha (1975, P-70) calls it Kaccā Bāhāḥ and Locke (1985, 174) mentions it as Bāchā Bāhāḥ. The ground floor sanctum is paved with stones and can be reached through a central door flanked by the statue of Gaṇeśa to the left and Mahākāla to the right. A stone statue of Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā is attached to the wall above the entrance of the sanctum, and a stone statue of Akṣobhya is enshrined in the sanctum. The Bāhāḥ's carved door frames are its original ones, though the door leaves have been replaced with more simple framed metal shutters. The fa&ccedil;ade of the two-storey building is completely covered with cement plaster and a simple band of cornice marks the separate floors. Three small cement lattice windows have been placed asymmetrically on the first floor. Four plain struts support the overhanging corrugated metal sheet roof. Most of the original architecture of the building can no longer be found in this structure. A ś ikharakūṭa c aitya (130 cm high) is located at the centre of the courtyard, the base of which is covered by the raised stone flooring. No historical record with which one could date Bāhāḥ could be made available. Pruscha (P-70) suggests that it was built some time around the 17th century. In 1971, the Bāhāḥ was renovated and this was funded by selling a piece of its land. Śākyas are the saṅgha members of the Bāhāḥ; two families perform its daily ritual worships. The responsibility for this is passed on between families every 15 days." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0700> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0700> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0740> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Chāya Bāhāḥcā (1659 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0740"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Three branches of Kvāḥ Bāhāḥ are located in Chāḥ Bāhāḥ ṭola . All of these branches are known as Chāḥ Bāhāḥ and so it is unclear which one is actually the Chāḥ Bāhāḥ. This Bāhāḥ is also called Bāhāḥcā in reference to its small size. It is located ca. 65 meters east of the Chāya Bāhāḥ Hiti. The courtyard of the Bāhāḥ can be reached by passing through a narrow alley and a carved door. The small courtyard measures 620 cm x 445 cm and houses an octagonal aṣṭakoṇaparivṛtākāra caitya . The main entrance to the Bāhāḥ is through a carved door, though there is also a plain door and a lattice window on its ground floor. Six statues flank the main door, with Khyāka, Gaṇeśa and Sāriputra to the left and Kavã, Mahākāla and Maudgalyāyana to the right. The statues of Khāka and Kavã are an interesting addition to this Buddhist monastic building, as they are more commonly found in the Śākta temples of the Valley. A pair of three praying wheels are fixed above the statues, and a wooden toraṇa with the images of Buddha, Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā and Lokeśvara is fixed above the door. The Bāhāḥcā has a cement-plastered fa&ccedil;ade and a pāsukhā jhyāḥ on its first floor. A stone statue of Akṣobhya is enshrined in its sanctum and an aniconic stone attached to the left wall of the building is worshipped as Bhairava. Relying on an inscription, Locke (1985, 44) dates the Bāhāḥ to NS 779 (1659 CE). A completely effaced stone inscription is attached to the wall of the building and is likely the one that Locke used for this. Four families take care of the Bāhāḥ, performing its daily ritual worships and other social-religious activities." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0740> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0740> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0780> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Duru Bāhāḥ Nani (1677 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0780"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Duru Bāhāḥ Nani can be accessed from the northwest corner of the Pimbahal area through a narrow alley under the residential buildings or via a narrow path to the west of the Sulima area. The main shrine has three storeys and faces east. The principal fa&ccedil;ade of the main shrine has a main door and two small latticed windows on the ground floor. A threefold pāsukājhyaḥ is on the central axis of the first floor, and has two small-carved windows on either side. Three openings can be found on the second floor above this, though they lack window shutters. The building has been reconstructed using bricks on mud mortar, and is roofed with corrugated metal sheets. Two d harmadhātu m aṇḍalas and a v ajradhātu c aitya are in line with the front of the main building. The courtyard was recently (writing in 2019) paved with hexagonal concrete blocks. Duru Bāhāḥ is a branch of the Kvāḥ Bāhāḥ (Hiraṇyavarṇa Mahāvihāra). Rather than referring to it as a vihāra or bāhāḥ , local people call it Bhagiṃ Nani. A historical photo (see Pruscha 1975, 148) shows that main shrine of the Bāhāḥ once had a tiled roof. Locke (1985, 128) has described the toraṇa above the entrance of the sanctum as containing images of Buddha, Dharma and Saṅgha, and Pruscha's photo (1975, 148) confirms this. However, the toraṇa is no longer there. Entering the sanctum, one finds an east-facing image of Akṣobhya. The inscription attached to the wall to the south of the entrance informs that this vihāra was established by Ratna Vajrācārya in NS 797 (CE 1677). Locke (ibid.) writes that it was a branch of Kvāḥ Bāhāḥ until 1975 and became later a branch of Guji Bāhāḥ. However, local people say that this Bāhāḥ is and has always been a branch of Kvāḥ Bāhāḥ. As evidence, they mentioned that a traditional practice takes place on the 10th day of the bright half of the Kārttika. On this day, the feast of the Kvāḥ Bāhāḥ Saṅgha is organised and the bāphāḥcā offers a pumpkin to its branch monasteries among them this Bāhāḥ." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0780> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0780> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0891> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Mati Bāhācā (1863 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0891"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Mati Bāhācā is a small courtyard containing two small monuments: one is a small shrine with Buddha&rsquo;s image, and the other is a pa&ntilde;cāvaliyukta caitya . There is another courtyard is on the route leading from Naka Bahī to Dhalāyacā; one can cross through this to reach the Mati Bāhācā courtyard. This courtyard is not in fact a bahāḥ. Rather, it derives its name from Mati Bahā, and the fact that it is small in size. In 1863 CE (NS 983), Govinda Siṃha and his wife Vidyā Lakṣmī established a pa&ntilde;cāvaliyukta caitya inside the courtyard in memory of their son Jitamāna. After this, the courtyard began to be known as Govindasiṃha Vihāra. Local inhabitants also call this place Cibhāḥ Cuka. A p ancāvaliyukta caitya typically has five layers, consisting of images of lotuses, water, jewels, vajra and fire, and this one does not deviate from this structure. On the top of these layers ( āvali s) is the sanctum ( garbha ) of the Caitya. Below this garbha one can find images of Buddha at each of the four cardinal directions. Five small images of Lakhinarasiṃha, Jitamāna, Matiju, Govindasiṃha and Vidyā Thakuṃ have been engraved on the Caitya&rsquo;s western plinth. There is an oil-lamp balustrade surrounding the Caitya, which spans over the RCC beams. These in turn are supported by short pillars. To the south of the Caitya is a small shrine made of stone with an image of Akṣobhya Buddha in its niche facing north. This shrine has been constructed on a plinth measuring 70 cm high and with a solid brick core. The Buddha image is housed in a smaller arched niche, also made of brick, which is covered by the stone slab roof of the stone temple. The stone temple structure itself measures 35 cm x 80 cm. The overall size of the temple is 150 cm x 120 cm, and it is 220 cm high. Its two-layered stone roof is decorated with lotus petals and crowned by a single &atilde;vaḥsāḥ stone gajū. This in turn is backed by a brick masonry wall with a width equal to that of the stone temple. To the north of this shrine is a pa&ntilde;cāvali caitya constructed on a plinth measuring 150 cm high on a square base of 116 cm. There is also a newly established shrine for Sarasvatī near this shrine. This courtyard is accessible from Mati Bāhā (to the west) through a narrow opening demarcated by two carved columns that are 120 cm in height. This route is open during matayāḥ and other such festivals." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0891> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0891> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0900> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Khāchẽ Bāhāḥ (1801 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0900"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This Bāhāḥ is located in Khāche&ntilde; Ṭola between Naka Bahī and Dhalāyacā Ṭola and is a branch of the Hiraṇyavarṇa Mahāvihāra. The stone-paved residential courtyard measures only 3 m x 3.2 m and can be accessed from the north, through an alley underneath a house connecting the courtyard with the Swotha street. The courtyard has a unique caitya at its center. It is one of a kind in Patan since its garbhag ṛ ha, harmikā, trayodaśabhuvana and pinnacle are all made of crystal. To the east of the Caitya, is a traditional door which still remains within the modern house fabric. The special carved timber door (missing the threshold) with an extended lintel must have been an entry to a special place related to the rituals but at present, the space is rented for commercial use. The shrine of the main deity of the Bāhāḥ is lost, and a space to the west is still empty waiting for the deity, an east-facing Akṣobhya Buddha, to return from the guthi member who took it to his home when the adjacent house was demolished. An inscription informs that Jayānanda built the Caitya in 1801 CE (NS 921). According to local informants, Jayānanda Śākya was a professional sculptor who used to make images of crystal. This crystal Caitya sits on a copper pedestal, which is shaped as a lotus with three folds of petals turned upwards. This lotus sits on a slim base that gently increases in circumference as it nears the ground. Beneath this is a double-tier rectangular stone plinth. The caitya is framed by a five-tier copper pinnacle, which is supported by four copper serpents, with their tails near the top and their heads facing downwards. It is encased in a metal cage and has a brass roof. Above the pinnacle is an unbrella ( chatra ) with five layers and which is supported by stylised snakes (nāga ) . There are four images in the four corners of the Caitya . In the southwest corner is an image of the donor (Jayānanda), and in the southeast corner is an image of Garuḍ a . Similarly, in the northwest corner there is an image of an elephant, and in the northeast corner there is an image of a lion." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0900> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0900> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0901> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Thyākā Bāhāḥ (1895 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0901"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Thyākā Bāhāḥ is located near Naka Bahī ( LAL0880 ) to the right-hand side of the street on the way to Kvālkhu. The small courtyard of the Bāhāḥ measures 3.6 m x 3.8 m and is surrounded by residential buildings. The way to access the Bāhāḥ is not immediately obvious; one needs to pass through a shop to reach it. The courtyard is encircled by a stone plinth and a cement plastered floor. The main temple is a single storey structure built on a raised plinth that is 80 cm high. It is open to the east side, while its rear west wall is attached to the neighbouring building. The main entrance to the shrine has a toraṇa attached to its timber carved frame and is sheltered by an overhanging roof, on top of which is half-domed pinnacle. The toraṇa displays images of Akṣobhya, Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā and Lokeśvara. Two small niches to the south and north of the main shrine house small statues of the Buddhist deities Ma&ntilde;juśrī and Padmapāṇi Lokeśvara respectively. Both niches are topped with prayer wheels. A small statue of Gaṇeśa can be found on the southern wall of the courtyard. The name of the Bāhāḥ is comes from the name of the ṭola . This is an independent Bāhāḥ ; it was constructed by a member of Hiraṇyavarṇa Mahāvihāra (Kvāḥ Bāhāḥ). Unlike other Bāhāḥs there is no kvāḥpāḥchẽ here. Instead, the shrine of Akṣobhya is attached to the western wall of the courtyard. Inside the sanctum, one can find a marble image of Akṣobhya sitting on two layers of lotus petals. Marble lions were placed on the both sides of the entrance. However, only the northern lion remains today (2019). Inside the courtyard, one can also find a djarmadhātujinālaya caitya (170 cm high). This was installed in 1895 (NS 1015). No inscription recording the history of the Bāhāḥ could be found." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0901> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0901> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0993> a hg:DhungeDhara ;
    rdfs:label "Amṛta Hiti" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0993"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Amṛta Hiti, locally known as Pvaḥ Hiti, was constructed during the medieval period for the Poḍhe community residing in Imūkhel, Lalitpur. The hiti has two water conduits, facing east. Generally, statues of Bhagīratha are installed under the water spout(s) of a hiti , however, this is not the case in Amṛta Hiti. A statue of Śrīdhara Viṣṇu is established on the hiti &rsquo;s northern wall which S. Khanal (2068, 173) dates to the sixteenth and seventeenth century. The source of water for this hiti originates from the hillock of Imūdvã. Due to rapid urbanization, the underground water level is depleting, hence the hiti dries up during winter and flows only during the monsoon season. There is no evidence to appoint the initial construction of the hiti . However, it is probable that it was built sometime during the medieval period ( Lalitpur Sub-Metropolitan City 2007, 45). It was renovated by the local community in 1988 CE (VS 2045). The UN-Habitat and Integrated Urban Development Project assisted to form a local committee in 2008 CE (VS 2065) for the management of water collection from the hiti during the night and distributing it to the public during the day. However, this plan did not work out. The local community cleans the hiti on the day of the Sithinakhaḥ, the sixth day of the bright fortnight of Jyeṣṭha." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0993> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0993> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0994> a hg:DhungeDhara ;
    rdfs:label "Vāsaḥ Hiti" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL0994"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Basaḥ Hiti is one of three hitis located next to each other at the base of the Imūdvã hillock in Lalitpur. The neighborhood is mainly inhabited by the members of Poḍe caste who . Therefore, the hiti is commonly used by them. The term vāsa ḥ means medicine in the Newari language, and thus, it is believed that water from this hiti contains medicinal properties. It is said that the Rāṇā rulers used to drink water from the Vāṣah Hiti ( Lalitpur Sub-Metropolitan City 2007, 42). The hiti has two water spouts. A statue of Bhagīratha is installed underneath the main spout (LAL0994-001) and a statue of Umāmaheśvara is placed above it. The date of construction of the hiti is not recorded but it is assumed that it was built during the reign of Prime Minister Juddha Śamśera Rāṇā [r. 1932-1945] ( Lalitpur Sub-Metropolitan City 2007, 39). A certain Jayarāja Śreṣṭha renovated the hiti in 1999 CE (VS 2056) and in 2011 CE (VS 2068) local residents, with the financial support from the Lalitpur Sub-metropolitan City, built a water storage and distribution system for the benefit of the local community. This system is still functioning. A minor renovation of the hiti took place in 2017 CE (VS 2074) when the hitis and phalcās in the Ālko quarter were repaired with funds from the World Bank and the Lalitpur Municipality. Unfortunately, little attention was paid to the use of authentic materials for the renovation. Due to rapid urbanization resulting in groundwater depletion, the flow of water in the hiti considerably decreases during the dry season. The local community cleans and worships the hiti on the day of the Sithinakhaḥ, the sixth day of the bright fortnight of Jyeṣṭha." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL0994> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL0994> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1250> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Taleju Bell (13th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1250"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This large bell hangs on a stone structure between two stone pillars and has a metal roof with gajura s. It was built by King Jayavishnu Malla and Queen Chandra Lakshmidevi and used for special proclamations by the Malla Kings. It is now rung during Khadgajatra in honour of the Goddess Taleju ( Pruscha 1975, 162). The Taleju Bell is located between the Hariśaṅkara Temple and Cyāsiṃ Devala in the Patan Darbar Square. Similar large bells also constructed during the Malla period can be found at Basantapur Darbar Square (Kathmandu) and the Bhaktapur Darbar Square. This bell hangs on a stone beam supported by two stone pillars that are erected on top of a platform. To reach the platform, one must enter the inside of the plateau through a narrow door to the east of the building and climb up a ladder with 9 rungs. Above the beam, one can see a gilded copper roof, and on top of the roof there are three pinnacles topped with parasols. Small wind chimes hang down from the bottom of the roof, which in turn is supported by 16 struts. Statues of śārdūlas can be found on the struts positioned in the four corners, while other struts contain carvings of Gaṇeśa, Kumāra, Bhairava and Mātṛkās. The body of the bell holds an inscription informing readers that it was commissioned by King Viṣṇu Malla (1729-1745) and Queen Candralakṣmī Devī. It also tells us that the work was supervised by general Mukunda Siṃha, and that this was paid for by the merchant Padma Siṃha. This inscription does not provide us any information about the intentions behind its construction. However, a Nepalese chronicle (see Bajracharya and Michaels 2016 , 129) states that the King installed this bell to scare his enemies. It is said that in the Malla period, this bell was rung to let people know that a royal announcement was about to be made; upon hearing the noise, people nearby would gather near the palace. It may also have been rung to tell citizens to come together for other special occasions. There is also a legend that the bell was rung daily in the morning and evening when the palace worshipped Goddess Taleju. At present, the bell may be sounded only during special occasions in the Dashain festival (for example when the demon-dancers come to dance in Mul Chowk and sacrifice animals). Only then is the small gate to the bell's platform opened." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1250> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1250> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1290> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Narasiṃha Mandira (1590 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1290"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Narasiṃha Mandira was built by Purandara Siṃha, a mah āpātra of Lalitpur in NS 710 (1590 CE) in memory of his elder brother Narasiṃha Deva. It is one of the few early śikhara towers in the Valley. Although Śaiva mortuary temples are found in many places, this is said to be the first mortuary temple with a Vaiṣṇava image ( Tiwari 2013, 113). Two stone lions guard the temple and two guarding deities are installed on the top plinth. The left deity is a four-handed Garuḍa using two hands to pray and the others to hold a flower and a water vessel. The deity sitting on the right-hand side is holding a cakra (wheel), aṅkuśa (hook), gadā and pāśa (noose). Inside the sanctum, an eight-handed stone image of Narasiṃha, the fourth incarnation of Viṣṇu, stands on a pedestal holding khaḍga , cakra , vajra and gadā with his right hands and śaṅkha , pāśa , padma and aṅkuśa with his left hands. Garuḍa sits to his left and Lakṣmī is located to his right. Unlike other commonly found Narasiṃha images, this Narasiṃha is standing but is not pictured tearing apart Hiraṇyakaśipu's abdomen. The temple sits on two lower plinths and an upper plinth with porches extending in all four directions. The sanctum tapers upwards towards the śikhara . Located on the ground floor, the main sanctum can be accessed from the east side by a series of steps. Stone pillars rest on either side of the porch, though the columns attached to the walls of the sanctum are made of timber. The lintel beam is also made from timber and has layers of brick cornice above it. Four doorways can be found on each of the sanctum's walls, but the main entrance is on the eastern side. Four porches have been built to form turrets above the sanctum, and are attached to the main śikhara tower. These turrets have small openings on three of their side walls, as well as terracotta toraṇa capped by decorative brick work and a stone finial. Their walls have been plastered with lime. The main brick tower is at the centre and contains seven bands known as saptaratha . The topmost crown of the śikhara is embellished with layers of floral patterned cornices and an āmalaka plastered with lime. The pinnacle is made from stone." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1290> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1290> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1341> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Garuḍa Stambha (1637 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1341"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "In 1637, King Siddhinarasiṃha Malla built this pillar in collaboration with the Kṛṣṇa Temple ( LAL1340 ). The gilded Garuḍa statue stands on an almost 7 meter tall rectangular pillar, measuring 55 x 55 cm. The 171 cm tall statue is greeting Kṛṣṇa in namaskāra gestur, and sits on a pedastal decorated with lotus petals. Whenever Kṛṣṇa is worshipped in the temple, the statue also receives some of the pūjā material, as Garuḍa is the vehicle of Viṣṇu. The image is therianthropic; half of it is in the shape of a bird, while the other half has a human appearance. Garuḍa appears in a benign posture; he is kneeling with his right knee upwards and his left knee on the pedastal as he prays to Kṛṣṇa. He is wearing a crown, and has a red ṭīkā on his forehead. Snakes adorn his neck and ears as ornaments, and he wears armlets on his wrists and arms. He has dhotī on his lower body and a girdle on his waist. Two wings and a tail can be found on his back." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1341> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1341> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1364> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Bhūkampa Smāraka (1941 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1364"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Bhūkampa Smāraka is a stone pillar that stands at the northern end of the Patan Darbar Square and in front of the three-tiered Bhīmasena temple (LAL1380) . The stone pillar is based on a double plinth of stone, which is surrounded by a square stone paving measuring 260 cm x 260 cm. The bottom plinth (17 cm high) measures 153 cm x 153 cm whilst the top plinth (25 cm high) measures 97 cm x 97 cm. The pillar is 168 cm high and has a 40 cm square base. The pillar is tapers at the base around a height of 70 cm; above this, it is straight. The top of the pillar has small recessed motifs and has a lotus base for an image of Annapūrṇā (58 cm tall). This pillar was erected in 1941 by the people of Lalitpur in memory of the devastating 1934 earthquake and in honor of Prime Minister Juddha Śamśera and his assistance. A similar kind of memorial was also established in the Bhugol Park of New Road, Kathmandu. The Patan Darbar Square pillar has an image of Annapūrṇā at its top. Standing upon a lotus pedestal, the goddess is holding a brahmada ṇḍa in her right hand and a water vessel in her left hand. She is wearing a crown and a long garland. Inscriptions have been written on the south and west faces of the pillar. One of these inscriptions records the destruction caused by the earthquake and the progress of the recovery work. In addition to this, it also details some of the development works undertaken by Juddha Śamśera. The inscription states that many people were helped by Juddha Śamśera, who donated 1 million 74 thousand and 913 rupees. It also records the amount of money collected in the assistance fund and its expenditure." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1364> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1364> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1372> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Mūchẽ Āgama (ca. early 15th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1372"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Mūchẽ Āgama is located to the north of the Bahādur Śāha. The building can be accessed through a small door opening on the western boundary wall. The Āgama was located at the south of a small courtyard facing north. The main entry door on the ground floor can be reached by climbing up on to a raised plinth guarded by two small stone lions. The main carved door is flanked by two small blind windows on either sides. A small secondary entry door on the principal fa&ccedil;ade (the eastern end of the building) leads to a stairway. There were two three-fold pasukā jhyāḥ on the first and second floor, both of which were salvaged after the earthquake. The historical name of the Āgama is Maṇichẽ, meaning a house located in the Maṇigala Ṭola. The word maṇichẽ has changed into mūchẽ over the course of time. This building appears to be a normal residential building, however, this is in fact the āg&atilde;chẽ of the Jośīs of Bakhumbāhāla and Kisis (Śreṣṭha) of Ombāhāla. The Rājopādhyāyas of the Sv&atilde;tha Ṭola perform the daily ritual worship here. Manamāneśvarī, the lineage deity of Licchavi kings who was worshipped before Taleju was brought to the Valley, is also worshipped inside this shrine. The Āgama used to house a collection of the swords of kings. When a ruling king died, his sword was preserved here. Jośī (2065, 16-17) states that there were once more than 30 swords inside this Āgama. The three-storey building of the Āgama was heavily damaged in the 2015 earthquake and now only the front wall of the ground floor remains. The main deity has been relocated to a neighboring house and various small images have been preserved in the Patan Museum. No historical record confirming the initial construction of the shrine is available. A Gaṇeśa statue located to the north-west was established in 1434 (NS 558). This provides grounds to assume that the Āgama may also have been constructed around this time, or may even pre-date the statue." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1372> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1372> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1540> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Ī Bahī Vãtā Vihāra (ca. 17th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1540"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Ī Bahī V&atilde;tā Vihāra, also named Sy&otilde; Bahī (&quot;ruined monastery&quot;), is located to the north of Yampi Mahāvihāra in front of the northern Aśoka Stūpa. The courtyard with three shrines and three caityas is supposed to be a branch of the Yampi Mahāvihāra. The Bahī area measures 21m x 30 m. The sunken courtyard is 82 cm below floor level, paved with flagstone tiles and measuring 10 m x 24 m. It is also home to three caitya s . The southernmost Caitya is a v ajradhātu caitya , while two other are śikharakūṭa caityas. To the east of the sunken courtyard, one can find three roofed shrines for the deities each Caitya facing west. The three structures are similar and measure 3.3 x 3.8 m. Local inhabitants worship these three small shrines as three different vihāras . Inside the shrines are three newly made stone images of Akṣobhya Buddha. Pruscha (1975, 209) and Locke (1985, 205) wrote about this monument, and it seems that some remnants of the vihāra were still present at their time. However, there are some contradictions in their descriptions. Pruscha writes that bronze images of Dīpaṅkara were enshrined in the temple sanctum dated 1672 CE (NS 792). Locke states that &quot;the northernmost shrine contains an image of Akṣobhya flanked by an image of Praj̃nāpāramitā on his right and one of Avalokiteśvara on his left; the central shrine has an image of Akṣobhya flanked by Padmapāṇi Lokeśvara on his right and Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā on his left. The southern shrine has a headless image of Akṣobhya&rdquo;." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1540> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1540> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1630> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Buramāṃ Vaidya Bāhāḥ (1866 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1630"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "The Buramāṃ Vaidya Bāhāḥ is a branch of Cik&atilde; Bahī ( LAL1620 ) and lies to its north. The courtyard is surrounded by residential housing and can be entered through the gates of a private house. The dyaḥchẽ (god house) is located on the south of the courtyard with its sanctum on the ground floor. An oil-lamp balustrade is fixed on the plinth of the dyaḥchẽ . A torana above the entrance displays Buddha, Nāmasaṅgīti and Ṣaḍakṣarī Lokeśvara. The sanctum's carved central door is flanked by plain lattice windows and small statues of Gaṇeśa and Mahākāla have been placed in the ground floor wall niches. In the sanctum statues of Buddha, Nāmasaṅgīti and Ṣaḍakṣarī Lokeśvara rest on stone pedestals. A statue of Ma&ntilde;juśrī is enshrined inside a room to the right of the sanctum and a statue of Vasundharā resides in the room to the left of the sanctum. The houses on the west and north sides of the courtyard are built in traditional Newar style with colonnaded ground floors and carved triple-bay windows on their first and second floors. These openings have cusped arches and can be dated to the early Saha period. Both houses have two new additional floors on top. The house on the east has been completely rebuilt in modern style. According to a local resident, all of the houses were once owned by the same family and used to be interconnected, however, they were later divided among the brothers. The surfaces of these houses are a particularly interesting feature of this courtyard. Each of the plastered surfaces of the walls facing into the courtyard has been decorated with wall paintings. The surfaces seem to have been made from a mixture of mud, rice husk and cow dung. Some of the wall paintings depict various deities, while others (such as the wall of the western corner of the god-house) are covered in verses in Sanskrit, most probably excerpts from the Jātaka tales (see photo: LAL1630_I_20191006_01). Most of the painted surfaces have eroded and only a few traces of the paintings remain intact today. A photo printed in Locke (1985, 213) shows that the house on the eastern side also had similar paintings and windows, however since its reconstruction it no longer has these features. On the wall paintings are depictions of various deities, some from Jātaka tales, Locke (1985, 214) maintains that the Bāhāḥ was founded in NS 986 (1866 CE) without providing a source. A stone inscription on the vedi of the northern face of the dharmadhātu jinālaya caitya mentions that Maṇiratna Vaidya enshrined the images of Triratnas in the sanctum in NS 998. Three days afterwards, the naming ceremony of the monastic building took place, where it was given the name &quot;Maṇikūṭa Vihāra&quot;. The families affiliated with the guṭhī of the monastic building were professional vaidya s (practitioners of Ayurvedic medicine); the monastic building is therefore also known as Vaidya Bāhāḥ." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1630> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1630> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1650> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Bhaiṣajyarāja Bāhāḥ (1878 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1650"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Bhaiṣajyarāja Bāhāḥ, a branch of the Cik&atilde; Bahī (Saptapura Mahāvihāra, LAL1620 ), is located behind a five-bayed phalcā at the junction of the street from Bhelāchẽ and Bhindyaḥ Sataḥ at Cyāsal. The entrance to this Bāhāḥ is on a boundary wall to the west and it is attached to a phalcā to its south. The main structure is divided into three small rooms, consisting of a central sanctum room and two small rooms on either side. A small staircase in the eastern room leads up to the upper floor. The building shares a small space with a single-storey annex structure to its north. The courtyard measures 7 m &times; 4.7 m, and has a residential building to the its east and north. Both of these are built on arcaded phalcās that serve as the ground floor. A caitya sits to the north of the Bāhāḥ, directly in front of its main door. The courtyard is paved with telia tiles. Small images of different Buddhist deities have been placed on the walls of the southern annex building and western boundary wall. Jīvanṛsiṃha, a member of the Cik&atilde; Bahī and an Āyurvedic doctor (vaidya) by profession, established this Bāhāḥ in 1878 (NS 998). It takes its name from Jīvanṛsiṃha's profession; hence, it is known as Bhaiṣajyarāja Bāhāḥ. Wall paintings of the Five Buddhas can be found above the plain outer entrance , which is flanked by paintings of auspicious symbols. The north-facing god house has two storeys and a tiled roof supported by plain struts. Instead of a pinnacle, a small chimney ( bhaipvāḥ ) can be found on its sloping roof, as well as three metal banners displaying images of deities. The first floor of the god house is very low. There is no toraṇa above the carved door of the sanctum, though it is flanked by two stone lions as it is customary. The lions also sit next to two metal flags that were donated in 1909 (NS 1023). The main facade of the god-house was decorated with wall paintings of various deities, but the old plaster is now crumbling away, making most of the paintings unrecognizable. Inside the sanctum, one can find a north-facing stone image of Akṣobhya." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1650> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1650> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1670> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Hona Bāhāḥ (ca. 1895 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1670"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Hona Bāhāḥ, also known as Hoḍola Vihārā or Kṛṣṇavīra Vihāra, is a one-storey god house located southeast of the Cyasal Square. This independent bāhāḥ was established by Kṛṣṇavīra Prajāpati (Tuisiṃ Āḥju) and is sometimes also referred to as a nani . The front facade resembles a three-bayed phalcā and the sanctum is located behind the central bay. The main statues in the sanctum are Akṣobhya, Tārā and Ṣaḍakṣarī Lokeśvara. The original Akṣobhya was stolen and replaced with a new one. The northern wall of the sanctum is furnished with latticed woodwork. Above the carved entrance door is a wooden toraṇa with Akṣobhya at the centre, Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā to the left and Lokeśvara to the right. Statues of Gaṇeśa, Mahākāla are attached to the front facade and a statue of Ma&ntilde;juvajra resides in a niche on the west interior wall. The roof is covered with traditional tiles. Hona Bāhāḥ is nestled between residential buildings on the west and east, two doors lead to a small courtyard to its south and the main facade faces an rectangular courtyard with a row of residential buildings. A small pa&ntilde;cāvali caitya was established in front of the main sanctum in 1895 CE (NS 1015). There are no records when the bāhāḥ was built, however, it is likely that it was established at the same time as the caitya . Hona Bāhāḥ was reconstructed with different bricks and types of mortar: the sanctum was rebuilt in dacīapā using cement mortar while the other walls consist of māapā , dacīapā and desiapā in mud mortar." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1670> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1670> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1690> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Kularatna Vihāra (ca. 1880 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1690"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Kularatna Vihāra is a branch of Cik&atilde; Bahī. It can be accessed via a narrow alley that runs under a residential building on the way to Cyāsal from Bhelāchẽ. The west-facing god house has a carved door, but lacks a toraṇa. There are no stone lions in front of the door and even the panels of the door are not fixed in place. A stone image of Akṣobhya can be found inside the sanctum - this small image is a replacement of the previous one, which was stolen in the 1960s. To the left of Akṣobhya, one can see a stone statue of a six-armed Ma&ntilde;juvajra. He holds a sword and an arrow in his two right hands and performs varada with the third one. His three left hands can be seen holding a book of the Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā, a lotus flower and a bow. Tārā sits to the right of Akṣobhya, and is performing varada and abhaya and holding lotus stalks in her two hands. To the left of Tārā, one can find a terracotta image of Red Matsyendranātha. His body is covered with a red cloth. According to local informants, this particular image was installed around 40 years ago. This vihāra is attached to the eastern boundary wall of a courtyard that measures 23 m x 8 m. The courtyard can be accessed through an opening on its western wall, which also contains images of Bhīmasena and Gaṇeśa. The building is a simple one-storey structure, and its back wall shares the courtyard's boundary wall. The three other sides are partitioned with timber lattice work. The main door is to the west, and is a swing door with metal vertical rails. The building is raised above the level of the courtyard in that it sits on a stone plinth of 1.8 m x 2.4 m. It appears that the plinth was recently covered with a concrete flat slab that is supported by brick piers on the western side. The roof has a stūpa finial that has been built with bricks on cement mortar. In front of the sanctum, one can find a small dharmadhātu maṇḍala . The western residential building in front of the vihāra has some remaining traditional wall paintings on the first floor level, though these are rapidly deteriorating. No inscription could be found to provide information about the establishment of this vihāra . However, Gellner (1987, 405) mentions that the nearby Bhaiṣajya Vihāra was established by Jīvanṛsiṃha in 1878 (NS 998). He claims that his eldest son established this Kularatna Vihāra some years later." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1690> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1690> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1700> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Śaṅkhadhara Bāhāḥ (1868 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1700"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Śaṅkhadhara Bāhāḥ is an independent vihāra . The building is located on the right-hand side of the street that leads from Bhelāchẽ to Cyāsal Square, and is easily identifiable thanks to the three phalcās that surround it. One phalcā sits in front of the monument (to the west), the second is attached to the southern side of the building, and the third can be found to its north just across the street. The Bāhāḥ sits on a high plinth of 75 cm; this has been built using unusually large bricks. The decorative kasimvaḥ and libī bricks are also larger than is characteristic for a bāhāḥ . The sanctum can be accessed by climbing a series of steps that are flanked by two stone lions. The building has a single carved door on the ground floor of the main facade, which faces west. The door is guarded by a pair of terracotta serpents with a decorative brick lining. The upper floor is separated by a band of timber cornices with a pair of lhāḥkā at each of their projected ends. On the first floor, a carved window is set in the center of the main wall and has two small blind windows on either sides. The original struts, the toraṇa and the roof structure are all missing. A new floor has been built on top of the first floor using brick masonry and mud mortar. This floor has a double-leaf glazed window. The building is now covered in corrugated metal sheets. The front yard is paved in stone. It has a caitya (225 cm high) on a square brick base and a dharmadhātu maṇḍala lined up on its western axis. This independent vihāra was established by Śaṅkhadhara Prajāpati in 1868 (NS 988). The carved, brass entrance of the sanctum has no toraṇa, but it does have the usual two stone lions sitting in front of it. The main image of Akṣobhya has been lost, and since then the sanctum of the Bāhāḥ has remained empty. A photo printed in Pruscha (1975, 176) shows stone pillars erected on the left-hand side of the plinth. These pillars are meant to hold a bell, however this too is missing. The pillars are also no longer there. To the left of the entrance, a small image of a four-handed Gaṇeśa has been placed in a small niche in the wall. Below this image, a stone inscription informs readers of the establishment of the Bāhāḥ. To the right of the entrance, one can find a small iamge of Kumāra riding on a peacock. An inscription mentions that Śaṅkhadhara Prajāpati established a guṭhī and provided it with land for the management of this Bāhāḥ. These days, however, the Bāhāḥ seems to be somewhat neglected. The ritual worship is also no longer performed." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1700> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1700> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1710> a hg:DhungeDhara ;
    rdfs:label "Ṭīkā Bhairava at Bhelachẽ (c. mid-16th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1710"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Measuring 367 x 367 cm in size, the west-facing Ṭīkā Bhairava temple is located in the Bhelāchẽ Tol, to the north of the Durbar Square of Patan. Ṭīkā Bhairava is also known as Unmatta Bhairava or Mahā Bhairava. The Bhelāchẽ Tol was named after this temple; its name derives from the Nevārī word bhelā, meaning Bhairava, and 'hẽ, meaning house. The main entry gate to the temple is to the west and is flanked by two guardian lions, which in turn have two metal flags between them. In front of the main entry, one can find a brick-paved space that contains several small objects. One of these is a stone Padmapatra, sitting almost at the centre and in line with the main entrance. One can also find a stone trough of an abandoned jarḥũhiti , which measures 50 cm in width and 173 cm in length. The lower banner on its right-hand side is broken. The Ṭīkā Bhairava temple is a three-storey pagoda-style temple, and stands on a low stone base. All three roofs of the temple are made of copper sheets. Above the entrance is a garuḍa toraṇa showing an image of Bhairava with eight hands. Bhairava is holding gajacarma, ḍamaru, katṛ and kapāla with his right hands, and gajacarma, khaṭvāṅga and naramuṇḍa with his left. The last hand is in a bindu position. Bhairava is at the centre of the toraṇa and is flanked by Gaṇeśa and Kumāra. Behind the toraṇa is a window with a wall painting of Khyā on its left and an image of Kav&atilde; on its right. Below the second roof, in front of the window on the western side, one can find a wooden toraṇa with images of similar deities painted in different colors. At the top of the temple is a pinnacle with serpents in its four corners. A banner hangs down from the pinnacle to the bottom of the temple's first roof on the western side. The sanctum is a colonnaded space with 12 octagonal columns standing in three bays on each side. The central area within this space is demarcated by a 2.1m long wall of 70 cm thickness and two timber posts. Together, these form a square space; the main image of the deity, which faces to the west, is attached to the wall. However, the sanctum does not contain a stone image of the main deity. Instead, there is a metal śivaliṅga , with an image of Bhairava painted on the wall behind it. To the left of the śivaliṅga is a stone image of Gaṇeśa with four arms, and to its right hand side is a stone image of Kumāra, also with four arms. Uutside, the temple has twenty struts supporing the lowest roof. The four corner struts contain images of śārdūla , while the other 16 struts display images of the Eight Mātṛkās and Eight Bhairavas. Alongside this, the struts to the west also contain images of Rudra Bhairava mounted on a human being, Māheśvarī, Unmatta Bhairava mounted on a lion, and Brahmāyaṇī. The southern struts display Bhairava mounted on a human being, Mahālakṣmī, a Bhairava riding on a horse, and Cāmuṇḍā. To the east, the struts contain images of Bhairava mounted on a bull, Indrāyaṇī, Kapāla Bhairava riding on a snake, and Vārāhī. Finally, the northern struts display images of Bhairava mounted on a Garuḍa, Vaiṣṇavī, Pracaṇḍa Bhairava mounted on a boar, and Kumārī ( Tachikawa 2004,17-19). In the temple area are a bell, a jarũhiṭī , a small stone image of Basāhā and an aniconic stone image of Ajimā. According to Pruscha (1975, 176) this temple was built in 1541 but no inscription could be found to provide evidence of this date. Mention of this temple is, however, made in Kīrtipatākā , a text composed by Kunu Śarmā. It describes images of gods drawn on the walls of the temple (see in Naraharinātha 1961, 12)." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1710> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1710> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1730> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Cī Bāhāḥ Nani (ca. 17th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1730"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Cī Bāhāḥ Nani is located to the east of the street that runs from Cyāsala to Olakhu. It is known as Ci Bāhāḥ Libi (a courtyard with a caitya ) amongt the local residents. The main facade of the structure has two columns that support a door screen between them. The door has a toraṇa above it and a metal screen to protect the image housed inside the sanctum. The roof has a plastered coping on the boundary wall and a small pinnacle. A cornice band runs along the boundary wall. Although Nanis are often associated with their main Bāhāḥs, this Nani has no association with any other major Bāhāḥ. It solely belongs to the Prajāpati family. Locke (1985, 72) assumes that Nani's present shrine was constructed after the 1934 earthquake. The shrine lacks many major features that are typical to a bāhāḥ . The main shrine is attached to a boundary wall on the south. The sanctum holds a north-facing stone statue of Akṣobhya wearing a lhegā (metal attire). Following the theft of the original statue a few years ago, this new statue is now protected by a metal grill. Broken pieces of glass have been embedded in the boundary wall for further protection. Above the door, one can find a toraṇa with a lotus petal in its centre. A stone relief depicting the Five Buddhas is placed underneath the image of Akṣobhya. The inscription on the pedestal of these images is now illegible. Images of Nārāyaṇa, Vajradhara, Ma&ntilde;juśrī, Padmapāṇi Lokeśvara, Gaṇeśa, and Mahākāla have been placed in a line on the wall. Outside, a Śikharakūṭa Caitya sits in front of the main shrine. The courtyard is paved with concrete tiles. The earliest inscription mentioning this Nani is dated to NS 796." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1730> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1730> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1900> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Tana Bāhāḥ" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1900"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Tana Bāhā located in Caṃki Ṭola, is a branch of Oku Bāhāḥ ( LAL2260 ). It has no proper shrine to house its principal deity ( kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ ) Akṣobhya Buddha. Therefore, the deity is kept inside a simple cement plastered structure (locally called kvāḥpādegaḥ ) which is capped by a caitya . A dharmadhātujinālaya caitya is established in the courtyard. No documented evidence is so far traced out to date the Bahā&rsquo;s initial construction. A narration is circulated amongst the local residents that it was built by certain Śreṣṭhas (Cipālu). The Bāhāḥ got destroyed due course of time and turned into ruins, which was then converted into a female toilet. Once upon a time, a person called Vidyālala Tāmrākāra experienced with his extraordinary sniffing capacity that something important and archeologically valuable is buried underneath the toilet (cf. H. Śākya 1994, 124). The Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s remnants were found after the site was excavated and it was then rebuilt by Harṣa Bahādura Śākya, a resident of Oṃ Bāhāḥ in NS 1069 (1948 CE). After it was restored, he provided this place for the nuns who practise Sthavīravāda (vernac. Therāvāda) Buddhism. It is called Jayamaṅgala Vihāra since then. The Bāhāḥ is still inhabited by such nuns and the decedents of Harṣa Bahādura Śākya have been performing the daily rituals here. [1] Footnotes ^ Although the ritual is called daily &lsquo; nitya&rsquo; thus needs to be performed every day, they perform it only after certain intervals." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1900> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1900> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1990> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Guitaḥ Caitya 1 (built before 13th century)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL1990"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Guitaḥ Caitya 1 is located in the vicinity of the Mū Guitaḥ Bahī ( LAL1980 ). It is built on a circular plan with four niches attached to the drum at each of the cardianal directions. Statues of the Four Buddhas have been placed in these niches. The main deity, Akṣobhya is placed in the southern niche with a lotus in front of him. The diameter of the single plinth drum is 670 cm, and the dome on of it has a diameter of 565 cm. Above the dome is a harmikā based on a square plan measuring 193 cm in width and 110 cm in height. There are thirteen steps or tiers above the harmikā and a ring of āmalaka around the central pillar ( yaṣṭi ). The finial is covered by a gilded parasol ( chatra ) with a pinnacle. A date (NS 368) has been inscribed on the stone pedestal. For safety reason, the statue has been cased inside an iron fence. A stone mandaḥpāḥ lies on the ground in front of the statue. The image of Amitābha that was previously established inside the western niche was stolen in 1984, and a new image of the same deity was installed immediately after this. A statue of Amoghasiddhi is enshrined on the northern niche. The eastern niche of the Guitaḥ Caitya 1 houses a broken statue of Buddha, probably Ratnasambhava. Its right hand and its left thumb are both fragmented. L. Bangdel (1995, 229) has dated this statue back to ca. 13th century. The caitya is painted in white lime and its dome is topped with a harmikā upon which the vajradṛṣṭi eyes and ūrṇā have been painted. These paintings can be found on all four sides; they symbolise nobility and illumination." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL1990> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL1990> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2020> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Guitaḥ Caitya 2" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2020"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Guitaḥ Caitya 2 is located to the east of the Cīdh&atilde; Bahī ( LAL1970 ) and to the north of the Guitaḥ Hiti. It is built on a circular plan with four niches attached to the drum at each of the four cardinal directions. The platform is paved with teliā tiles and has a stone edge. A fire alter can be found in the courtyard in front of the shrine. The main deity is Akṣobhya and his statue has been placed in the western niche. This is unusual, as he is normally located on the eastern side of such caitya. Here, however, he faces towards the main deity in the Cīdh&atilde; Bahī. This main western niche is adorned with a stone doorframe displaying images of the Five Buddhas. The other three niches are plain and exhibit a different type of roof. The diameter of the single-plinth drum is 790 cm and the dome atop it has a diameter of 720 cm. Above the dome is a harmikā based on a square plan of 187 cm and measuring 100 cm in height. There are thirteen rectangular tiers and an āmalaka and a yaḥsĩ (central pillar) above the harmikā, and a gilded parasol has been fixed above the caitya . A statue of Ratnasambhava undeneath five nāgas (snakes) is housed in the southern niche of the Caitya. These five nāgas intertwine to form a triangular shape above him. Amitābha is enshrined in the eastern niche and Amoghasiddhi is located in the northern one. The images of Amitābha and Ratnasambhava seem to be have been added recently. These four shrines have been topped with caitya -shaped pinnacles, though the pinnacle of the northern niche is damaged. The main Caitya is surrounded by four smaller khaṭa- style caityas at its four corners; this formation is known as cila ̃dyaḥ. These caityas have been painted with white lime and house images of Buddha. Images of Padmapāṇi Lokeśvara have been enshrined inside the north-western and north-eastern caityas . An image of a six-handed deity standing on a yoni has been enshrined in the south-eastern caitya . The deity&rsquo;s face, as well as the number of his hands and weapons cannot be seen clearly. He might be one of the Lokeśvaras. Inside the south-western khaṭa -shaped caitya, one can find a sitting statue of Vairocana alongside his associative deities. L. Bangdel (1995, 228) has dated the Vairocana image to around the 6th century. All four caitya s have small caitya shaped pinnacles at their top. The pinnacles of the north-western and south-eastern structures have been damaged; only the āmalaka part of the remains. The thirteen tiers of the north-eastern caitya have also been lost. No inscription or any other documented evidence could be found to provide information about the establishment of the caitya . Since the Vairocana image on the south-western khaṭa -shaped caitya has been dated to the 6th century, it can be assumed that the main Caitya was also constructed around that time." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2020> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2020> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2030> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Guitaḥ Gaṇeśa Mandira" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2030"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Guitaḥ Gaṇeśa Mandira (Temple) is located behind the waterspout at the Guitaḥ Ṭola, and faces west. Generally, each Newar city quarter ( ṭola ) in the Kathmandu Valley has one Gaṇeśa temple, but there are two at the Guitaḥ Ṭola. The Guitaḥ Gaṇeśa Temple was constructed from the Guṭhī comprising Śākyas and Maharjana caste members of the Ṭola. This one-storey Temple stands on a relatively tall plinth and is tiled with jhĩgaṭīs . The roof is capped by a metal pinnacle which is flanked by a pair of metal flags. A long metal banner ( patāḥ ) connected to the base of the pinnacle is hanging down up to the eves board. The door to the sanctum is capped by a carved wooden toraṇa and is flanked by a pair of lions. A dancing Gaṇeśa image is carved on the middle of the toraṇa and a Buddha image is carved on either side of Gaṇeśa. The sanctum of the Temple houses a stone statue of Gaṇeśa whose head is capped by a metal crown ( mukuṭa ) . L. Bangdel (1995, 230) has dated this statue to the 18 th century CE. There are some other statues established on the plinth of the Temple. Among them, the Śiva statue (LAL2030-010), which Bangdel (1995, 229) has identified as Viṣṇu, is said to be from the 14 th century CE. The statue of Viṣṇu (LAL2030-012) is dated to 15 th Century CE, and the statue of Umāmaheśvara (LAL2030-009) is said to be from the 17 th century CE (cf. Bangdel L. 1995, 229). Moreover, there is also a Sarasvatī statue (LAL2030-011) established in 1775 CE (NS 895) by a certain monk īndrajyoti. We have no historical sources to date the initial construction of the Temple. In 2008 CE (VS 2065), it was reconstructed with the financial support from the Lalitpur Municipality. The above mentioned Guṭhī currently (as of June 2021) has nine members (two Śākyas and seven Maharjanas). On the day of the Gũlā Pāru (the first day of the bright fortnight of Bhādra), the Guṭhī members place a brass Gaṇeśa statue in front of the main statue inside the sanctum for public display and worship it. A similar ritual is conducted also on the day of the Indra Jātrā Festival. On this day, samaybaji is offered to the deity." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2030> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2030> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2080> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Dathu Bāhāḥ (c. 1880s CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2080"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Dathu Bāhāḥ is a member of the branch of Mayūravarṇa Mahāvihāra and is situated southwest of this monument. It is one of three bāhāḥs that are located near to the Mayūravarṇa Mahāvihāra at its southeast corner. Dathu is positioned in between these, which is how it gained its name. The Bāhāḥ can be accessed through an underpass underneath a residential building from the western Cidhaṃ Bāhāḥ ( LAL2070 ). This Bāhāḥ has been built on the eastern wing and faces west. At present, however, it consists only of a ground floor, and this is also in ruins. Before 2015, this site was a three-storey Bāhāḥ complex. Three plain windows were installed on its first floor, and a secret room was located on this level. The second floor contained a trikhājhyāḥ flanked by two plain windows. The roof of the Bāhāḥwas made from zinc sheets. However, the first and second floors collapsed during the 2015 earthquake. Although the ground floor was badly damaged, the sanctum of the Bāhāḥ has remained safe. The ground floor has three colonnaded bays on its main facade; the central bay holds the main door. The space between the columns on the right-hand side is filled with brick masonry, while a small entry way can be found on the left. The architecture shows that this Bāhāḥ has stood witness to different periods of time. The main door is well carved and once was surmounted by a ṭoraṇa th&atilde; held up by stone lions, however the ṭoraṇa was removed after the earthquake. Historical photos show that the first and second floor contained a single opening window and a triple-bay window ( svapājhyaḥ ) respectively. The back wall (to the east) has been heavily damaged and parts of it have collapsed. The main sanctum room has been covered with CGI sheets to protect it from rain. The courtyard (8.95m x 7.25m) is paved in telia tiles, and contains a Dharmadhātu Maṇḍala and a Śikharakūṭa Caitya (160cm high) lined up in the central axis in front of the main sanctum (to the west). The courtyard is still filled with debris from the collapsed building materials. Inside the sanctum, the stone statue of the seated main deity, Akṣobhya Buddha, faces west. The original statue was stolen some time between 1983-1985. Later, a similar statue was enshrined and covered with a metal garment. After the 2015 earthquakes, however, the metal garment of the deity disappeared. A small stone Caitya and a stone with sculpted images of four donors has also been preserved inside the sanctum. Before the earthquake, there was also a wooden tora ṇa dated NS 1004 with an image of Akṣobhya in the middle, Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā on the right and Ṣaḍakṣarī Lokeśvara on the left ( Locke 1985 , 87). After the earthquake, broken pieces of the tora ṇa were found scattered around, but the piece containing the date could not be located. The pinnacle which was once fixed on top of the toraṇa can now be seen above the entrance. Images of Hanumān and Mahākāla also survived the earthquake; they are now placed to the north and the south of the entrance." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2080> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2080> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2130> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Gaṇeśa Mandira" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2130"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "As it the case of most of the Gaṇeśa Mandiras in the Kathmandu Valley, this Mandira, too, faces, south. The local residents of Tyāgala Ṭola worship this Gaṇeśa as their area deity ( sthāna ). It is built in a pagoda-style and has three storeys. The main door to the sanctum is flanked by a pair of lions, and an artistically carved toraṇa with the images of Gaṇeśa (in the centre) and two people playing the musical instrument mṛdaṅga (in the left and right) is kept above the door. The images of Śiva, Gaṇeśa, and Mātṛkā Goddesses have been carved on the struts supporting the roofs. The last roof of the temple is metal tiled, however, the first and second roofs are tiled with jhĩgaṭī bricks. The photograph published by Pruscha (1975, p. 189) shows an additional pinnacle erected on the roof of the second floor. However, this pinnacle is not there anymore. No documented evidence is so far traced out which records the date of the temple&rsquo;s initial construction. The date inscribed on the toraṇa in Nepāla Calendar Era could be the date of the initial construction. However, since the middle numeral of this date is defaced, its exact dating remains obscure. Pruscha (1975, p. 189) has mentioned that a guṭhī was established in NS 768 (1648 CE) to sponsor the daily and occasion rituals for this temple. He also informs us that the struts supporting the roofs were carved in NS 863 (1743 CE). Since he does not validate this information through any documented evidence, its authenticity remains questionable. An inscription from NS 853 (1733 CE) is kept inside the sanctum. It records about the establishment of a guṭhī that is responsible to sponsor the rituals for the caitya erected in front of this Gaṇeśa Mandira. The Mandira was renovated in VS 2060 (2003 CE) by the Department of Archelogy (source: Local knowledge, a resident of the area, Maharjana, male, age 49). The specific iconographical features of the Gaṇeśa statue [1] of this temple suggest that it is one of the oldest Gaṇeśas from the Kathmandu Valley dating back from the 5 th to 7 th century. There are three separate guṭhī s of Jyāpus, Mulmīs, and Bārāhīs of Tyāgala Ṭola related to this temple. The Jyāpus&rsquo; guṭhī sponsors ritual worship to Gaṇeśa on the day called Picāśa Caturdaśī followed by an animal sacrifice. The Mulmīs&rsquo; guṭhī worship Gaṇeśa on the day of Indrajātrā procession and Piśāca Caturdaśī and displays a metal Gaṇeśa statue in the temple for the public, which is also followed by an animal sacrifice. The Bārāhī&rsquo;s gūṭhī too organises special worship for Gaṇeśa every year, on the day of Kuśe Aũśī followed by the sacrifice of a he-goat. In addition to these guṭhīs , a separate guṭhī of the Jyāpus of Tyāgala Ṭola too places a toraṇa above the main statue in the sanctum and displays it publicly twice a year. Footnotes ^ For example, it has two arms, a big belly, is not adorned with pieces of jewellery, is in the ardhaparyaṅka position, its trunk is slightly twisted to the right and it is not accompanied by other statues, such as his family members and transporter (see Khanāla VS 2074, 163 for the iconographical features of the old Gaṇeśa statues in the Kathmandu Valley)." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2130> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2130> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2160> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Pānda Bāhāḥ (1877 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2160"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Pānda Bāhāḥ is located at Ghavāḥ Nani in Lukhusi Ṭola. Unlike most of the bāhāḥs in Patan, this one belongs to the Maharjanas, and not to the Vajrācāryas or Śākyas. Therefore, it has neither a shrine ( kvāḥpāḥchẽ ) for the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s primary deity ( kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ ), nor a saṅgha. A small shrine is built in the south-western corner of the Ghavāḥ Nani. The bell-shaped shrine is painted in white, capped by a caitya -shaped pinnacle, and encircled by the serpents ( nāga ). A stone statue of Akṣobhya flanked by Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā and Lokeśvara is enshrined inside the shrine&rsquo;s sanctum sanctorum as kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ. A Pa&ntilde;cāvalīyukta Caitya, Gaṇeśa, and Mahākāla statue are installed in the Bāhāḥ premises. Pānda Bāhāḥ was built in NS 997 (1877 CE) by Kiṣṇa (text: Kṛṣṇa) Maharjana and his wife Tuyu (Lakṣmīdevī) living in Lukhusi Ṭola. They also established the Pa&ntilde;cāvalīyukta Caitya in the Bāhāḥ premises and formed a guṭhī to worship it in NS 1000 (1880 CE). The decedents of the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s founders (especially three families) are still performing the daily rituals. The anniversary celebration of the Bāhāḥ takes places every year, on the full moon day of Āśvina." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2160> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2160> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2230> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Yaṅgaḥ Bāhāḥ (built before 1039 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2230"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Yaṅga Bāhāḥ is a branch of the Rudravarṇa Mahāvihāra, Oku Bāhāḥ ( LAL2260 ). This Bāhāḥ faces east, and its roof is tiled with jhĩgaṭī . While the shrine for the esoteric deity ( āgaṃ ) is located on the first floor, the sanctum ( garbhagṛha ) is on the ground floor. A stone statue of Akṣobhya is the bāhāḥ&rsquo;s primary deity ( kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ ). This stone statue lost its authenticity, since it has been painted in enamel paint. A Śikharakūṭa Caitya in the couirtyuard which was renovated in NS 1063 (1943 CE). The bāhāḥ is mentioned in a palm-leaf manuscript from NS 159 (1039 CE). ( K&ouml;lver & Śākya 1985, 103). The Bāhāḥ had been abandoned for a long time, and consequently turned into ruins. The local elders mentioned that it was haunted even during the daytime. Since it was not possible to run the daily operations of the Bāhāḥ, its saṅgha members, Yogaratna Śākya, Munirāja Śākya, Teja Bahādura Śākya, and others, handed over the Bāhāḥ to a Bhikṣu Saṅgha (an organization of some monks who practice at the Therāvāda school of Buddhism) in VS 2000 (1943 CE). Later in VS 2007 (1950 CE), it came under the jurisdiction of this organization ( Śākya 2053, 3). Following the hand over of the bāhāḥ, it was named after a Sri Lankan monk, Sumaṅgala Vihāra by Nārada Mahāsthavīra ( Śākya 2053, 1). No one addresses henceforth the Bāhāḥ by its old name. Contemporary buildings surround the Bāhāḥ in the present-day, and it serves as a learning centre for the Therāvāda Buddhism." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2230> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2230> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2240> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Kani Bāhāḥ at Oku Bāhāḥ (1702 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2240"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Kani Bāhāḥ is a branch of Oku Bāhāḥ (Rudravarṇa Mahāvihāra, LAL2260 ) and was established in 1702 (NS 822) by Devarāja Śākya. It is located at the southeast corner of Lalitpur city, on the way to Sincāhiti from Oku Bāhāḥ. This Bāhāḥ is on the fringe of the city, at the crossroad of the roads from Prayag Pokhari to Gwarko and Satdobato. It can be accessed via a carved door on the northern side of the complex. The front facade of the main door has changed and shops have been built up around it. Two doors have also been added on either side of it. The main door is accentuated by two small blind windows and carvings of deities on either side of it. Entering the door via a vestibule, one finds a colonnaded space ( phalcā ) on the second bay, as well as images of Gaṇeśa, Mahākāla and Hanumān to the left, on the eastern wall, and an image of Bhīmasena to the right, on the western wall. The Phalcā has three bays and a pair of columns ( jvaḥthā̃ ). The main courtyard is surrounded by a plinth, measures 8.85m x 8.85m, and is 45cm below the level of the Phalcā. The main sanctum building is a three-storey structure built on the southern wing of the courtyard. It has a richly carved central main door on the ground floor that is flanked by two small windows with circular lattice work on either side. The upper floor is distinguished by a band of carved timber cornice that is typical to Newar architecture. On the first floor, one can find a pasukā jhyāh with two aedicules on either side. The second floor is mainly occupied by a central slanting projected window known as gā jhyāḥ . The sanctum building has two exit doors on the ground floor; the eastern door leads to an open space to the south, while the western one is the entrance to a residential building. There are two lattice windows on the first floor and one single window on the second floor. The roof is covered with jhiṅgati tiles and has a stūpa finial on its ridge. The building now stands in the shadow of a newly constructed five-storey building behind it, i.e. to its south. The courtyard is well paved with high quality teliyā tiles. It contains a Durgatipariśodhana caitya (220cm high), a statue of some donors and a dharmadhātu maṇḍala (80cm high). These are all lined up in front of the main sanctum building. The north-facing god house of the Bāhāḥ has three storeys, though it was seriously affected by the 2015 earthquake. It can be found at the southern side of the courtyard. Its worn tiled-roof is damaged in some parts, and vegetation is growing on and through it. Although the roof is dilapidated, it is still topped by a caitya -shaped pinnacle. According to Bāhāḥ members, Lalitpur Metropolitan City provided 28 Lakh Rupees for the reconstruction of the monastery." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2240> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2240> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2290> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Taḥdhãlibi Bāhāḥ (1598 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2290"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Taḥdh&atilde;libi Bāhāḥ can be accessed via a narrow alleyway that runs underneath a residential house, to the north of the way from Uku Bāhāḥ ( LAL2260 ) to Ubāhā Bahī ( LAL2280 ). The main shrine of the bāhāḥ is a plastered structure that is raised above the ground on a plinth. The roof of the temple has a stūpa as its finial, and the temple structure itself is decorated with pilasters at each corner. The main sanctum faces west, and its entrance is emphasised by the triangular motif that sits above it. A small dharmadhātu maṇḍala (45 cm high) and a vajradhātu caitya (145 cm high) that sits on a square base of 120 cm are both lined up centrally to the west of the main shrine. This ensemble of monuments can be found towards the south-eastern corner of the courtyard. The courtyard measures 11.7 m &times; 9.2 m and is paved in telia tiles. A narrow alley to the north-western corner leads on to the Dune Bāhāḥ ( LAL2300 ). Generally, this place is not referred to as a bāhāḥ ; instead, local residents call it a libi/coka (courtyard). Locke (1985) also does not include this monument in his list of vihāras . However, Śākya (1994, 117-18) counts it as a branch of Oku Bāhāḥ (Rudravarṇa Mahāvihāra, LAL2260 ). The structure of this vihāra differs from that of other vihāras . A cement-plastered shrine topped with a stūpa -shaped pinnacle stands on the eastern side of the courtyard. There are no traditional carvings nor decorative objects in this shrine. Nāmasaṅgīti is enshrined in the sanctum as the main deity, and images of Mahākāla and Gaṇeśa have been attached respectively to the northern and the southern walls of the shrine. The shrine can be circumambulated via a narrow passage. A largely effaced stone inscription stands at the southeast corner of the caitya . Śākya (1994, 117-18) has identified the date of the inscription as NS 718. However, the inscription is now mostly illegible and so the date could not be confirmed." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2290> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2290> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2300> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Dune Bāhāḥ (1659 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2300"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Dune Bāhāḥ, one of the branches of Oku Bāhāḥ ( LAL2260 ), is located in Okubāhāl Ṭola to the north of Oku Bāhāḥ. This Bāhāḥ can be accessed from two directions: either from the western side through a narrow alley between the houses opposite Mahābuddha Temple ( LAL2340 ), or from the south through another alley passing through an open courtyard. The monument building can be found on the west side of the courtyard and faces east. The principal fa&ccedil;ade has a main central door that is decorated with carvings of floral details and images of the Buddhist deities. The door is flanked by two narrow openings on either side. The ground floor of the building has been plastered in cement and painted white. It is separated from the first floor by a band of carved timber cornices. A pāsukājhyaḥ with three openings can be found at the centre of the first floor with two small arched windows on either sides. Above the central window, one can find a blind window with five arched recesses; these symbolise Pa&ntilde;cabuddhas. This floor was recently reconstructed using local deśī apā on mud mortar (writing in 2019). The structure is covered with a sloped CGI sheet roof, and is surrounded by tall, five-storey buildings on three sides. The courtyard contains a central Buddha shrine with three tiers (3.7m high), a Dharmadhātu Maṇḍala (50 cm high) and a Dharmadhātu Jinālaya Caitya (1.7 m high). This Bāhāḥ was established by Punaju Śākya of Oku Bāhāḥ in NS 779 (1659 CE), which is why it is also called Pūrṇacandra Vihāra. Earlier, the god house of the monastic building had three storeys, however it was severely damaged by the 2015 earthquakes. As a result, the third floor has since been removed, and a roof of corrugated zinc sheets has been installed on top of the second floor. The fa&ccedil;ade of the first floor has a trikhājhyāla with a carved toraṇa; this is flanked by two small windows. Above the window, five niches made from terracotta symbolise the Five Buddhas. The ground floor houses the sanctum, which contains an icon of an east-facing Akṣobhya sitting in a bhūsparśa gesture. Dharmacakra Ma&ntilde;juśrī sits to the left of the figure, whilst a statue of Avalokiteśvara can be found to its right. The entrance to the sanctum is flanked by two small windows but has no toraṇa . Instead, images of Akṣobhya Buddha, Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā and Lokeśvara can be found above the door. A statue of a four-handed Ganeśa is housed in a niche to the right of the entrance (which lies to the south). Above this, there is a toraṇa with a further image of Gaṇeśa. Another niche to the left of the entrance (to the north) contains an image of Mahākāla; like the one on the right-hand side, this too is surmounted by a torana, this time displaying an image of Mahākāla." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2300> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2300> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2330> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Ghana Bāhāḥ (ca. 1840 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2330"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Ghana Bāhāḥ is a branch of Oku Bāhāḥ (Rudravarṇa Mahāvihāra, LAL2260 ), is a small area surrounded by a historic, private building to the north, the Mahākāla Phalcā to the east, the Vajradhara Phalcā to the west and small buildings and a boundary wall to the south. Ghanānanda Śākya, a grandson of Amṛtānanda Vande (the man who taught Buddhism to Brian Hodgson), established this bāhāḥ and it is from him that it gets its name. The small, single-roofed domed shrine is made of bricks and contains a stone image of Akṣobhya in its sanctum. Akṣobhya is facing west, and there is a rounded dharmadhātu maṇḍala in front of him. The shrine itself is located between the two phalcā s and rests on a single plinth. Parts of the base and the dome have been plastered with lime and white-washed, and the walls are built of daci apa and decorative bricks. The stone doorway features an arch with the Five Buddhas carved on it. A well can be found to the south of the shrine. No historical document could be found to provide the exact date the bāhāḥ was constructed. However, it is possible to estimate a rough date with the help of sources that tell us about Ghanānanda's life more generally. Ghanānanda is mentioned in an inscription dated NS 955 ( Śākya 1994, 55-56). Furthermore, in NS 975 (1855 CE), on the first anniversary of his death, a dharmadhātu maṇḍala was established in front of the Mahābuddha Temple ( LAL2340 ) in his honour ( Śākya 1988, 88-89) . This suggests that the bāhāḥ was established before 1854 and very probably after 1835." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2330> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2330> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2350> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Bhājudhana Bāhāḥ" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2350"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Bhājudhana Bāhāḥ is located to the west of Uku Bāhāḥ ( LAL2260 ). It is located inside an L-shaped courtyard which can be accessed through a narrow underpass by a six storied building. The bāhāḥ is on the way to Thāpāhiṭī from Mahābuddha. The main monument building is a small structure attached to a residential building to the west. It is not visible from the main entrance; one must walk to the end of the long courtyard and turn right to find an adjacent courtyard. Together, they form an open L-shaped space. Bhājudhana Bāhāḥ is a branch of the Oku Bāhāh. The god house sits on a stone plinth raised above the ground. The main sanctum is constructed from timber and exhibits intricately carved door jambs and a toraṇa thā (the place for toraṇa ). Four corner posts have been carved to resemble the column that supports the three-tiered roofs, which in turn are covered with brass plates. The structure's pinnacle is missing. Behind the monument, there is a four-storey residential building with a projecting carved open window at the center flanked by two vertical lattice windows on either sides on the first floor. Above this, the second floor boasts a central and elaborately carved triple-opening window. The ground floor has a three-bayed colonnaded structure, and the central bay is occupied by this monument. In front of the monument, one can find a dharmadhātujinālaya caitya (2 m high) and a dharmadhātu maṇḍala (65 cm high) on a raised platform paved in stone. The structure of this bāhāḥ differs from other bāhāḥ of Patan; this bāhāḥ has no kvāḥpāḥchẽ . The brass-clad roof of the temple is supported by struts that display images of Lokeśvaras. Previously, one could find an image of Amitābha inside the temple's sanctum, but this is presently at the home of one of the main authorities of the bāhāḥ . A toraṇa above the entrance of the sanctum shows Lokeśvara with Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā to her right and another Lokeśvara to her left. Śākya (1994, 118-119) writes that the sanctum also contained wall paintings of Māyādevī, Cintāmaṇi Lokeśvara, Padmasaṃbhava, Harihariharivāhanalokeśvara, Uṣṇīṣavijaya, Āryatārā, Dhvajāgrakeyūrī and the first Bhīmarathārohaṇa. However, only three of them (Uṣṇīṣavijaya, Āryatārā and Padmasaṃbhava) are still there. Bhājudhana Śākya was a member of the saṅgha of Oku Bāhāḥ and he established this bāhāḥ with help of his elder brother Bhīmarāja and other family members. Bhājudhana had shop in Tibet where he sold gold jewellery. He established this vihāra using the money he earned from his business. As a result, this vihāra is also called Hiraṇyalābha Vihāra. In NS 1027 (1907 CE), Bhājudhana established a dharmadhātujinālaya caitya in the courtyard. Two years later, he made a small god house and enshrined the image of Akṣobhya inside it." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2350> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2350> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2360> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Bhīmarāja Bāhāḥ (1909 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2360"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Bhīmarāja Bāhāḥ is a branch of the Oku Bāhāḥ ( LAL2260 ). It can be accessed via the entrance of a private building that is located at the southwest corner of the Bhājudhana Bāhāḥ ( LAL2350 ). Bhīmarāja Śākya, a member of the saṅgha of Oku Bāhāḥ, established this bāhāḥ . The structure of the Bhīmarāja Bāhāḥ differs from other bāhāḥ of Patan. This bāhāḥ has no no esoteric god house, and its courtyard (5.2 m x 5.2 m) contans an uncommon caitya. Located at the very center of the courtyard, this caitya is the main monument of the bāhāḥ and has a structure that is not often seen in the Valley. Four stone pillars have been erected at each of the four corners above its double-layered lotus petals (which together are 96cm in diameter), supporting a layer of stone that forms a roof. A statue of Avalokiteśvara stands on top of the lotus petals, and a stūpa -shaped finial has been placed on the roof . Śākya (1994, 35) calls it a Kūṭāgārākṛti Caitya. It is based on a square plinth measuring 185cm in width and 13cm in height. The sanctum has iron railings fixed on three sides, with the western face left free. A small, west-facing marble image of Avalokiteśvara stands inside the sanctum. Adorned in a crown, a large necklace, and a dhoti, the deity is performing varada with his right hand and holding the stem of a lotus in his left. The courtyard is paved in telia tiles and a plinth surrounds the residential buildings. The three-storey building on the northern side has a colonnaded three-bay phalcā underneath it. No inscription or any other document could be found to confirm the consecration date of this bāhāḥ . However, Bhīmarāja, the elder brother of Bhājudhana, established the bāhāḥ just after his younger brother established the Bhājudhana Bāhāḥ in 1909 ( Śākya 1994, 120). Nowadays, some small cracks can be seen in the caitya. These were caused by the 2015 earthquake. Some aniconic stones images worshipped as Lukumāhādyaḥ have been placed at the northeastern corner of the courtyard. Śākya (1994, 121) mentions that a school named Mahābauddha Pāṭhaśālā once operated in this vihāra, and had 150 students. He also writes that there were wall paintings of Vīṇārāja, Khaḍgarāja, Dhvajarāja and Caityarāja on the wall of a house located to the south of the courtyard." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2360> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2360> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2400> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Vasu Bāhāḥ (built around 1938 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2400"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Vasu Bāhāḥ is a branch of Rudravarṇa Mahāvihāra, Oku Bāhāḥ ( LAL2260 ). Unlike other bāhāḥs in Patan and elsewhere, this Bāhāḥ does not have a proper shrine ( kvāḥpāḥchẽ ) for the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s primary deity ( kvāpāḥdyaḥ ). Instead of a kvāḥpāḥchẽ , a small temple is built in the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s courtyard (called kvāḥpāḥdegaḥ ), and a statue of Akṣobhya is enshrined inside its sanctum. The temple is plastered with cement and capped by a caitya as its pinnacle. A stone toraṇa on which the images of Five Buddhas (Pa&ntilde;cabuddha) have been carved, is installed above the entrance to the sanctum. An additional stone statue of Akṣobhya which is slightly bigger than the main one is installed behind the main statue of Akṣobhya in the sanctum. No one living in the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s premises knows about this additional Akṣobhya statue. A Dharmadhātu Maṇḍala has been established right in front of the kvāḥpādegaḥ&rsquo;s entrance. The inscription on the Dharmadhātu is broken, thus the date of its establishment can not be ascertained. Next to the kvāḥpāḥdegaḥ is another small temple, which houses some Amitābha statues. Therefore, local residents call it Amitābha Mandira. Further, Pruscha (P-240) erroneously mentions this temple (Amitābha Mandira) as the kvāḥpāḥdegaḥ of Vasu Bāhāḥ. No documented evidence has so far traced out to date the initial construction of Vasu Bāhāḥ. Śākya (1994, 110) mentions that leftover bricks after the renovation of Mahābuddha Mandira ( LAL2340 ) in 1938 CE (1058 NS) were used to construct this Bāhāḥ. Therefore, we can assume that it was built around that time. However, local residents claim that it was built before the 1934 CE (1990 VS) earthquakes. The structure of the kvāḥpāḥdya and Amitābha Mandira also suggest that this Bāhāḥ is relatively new. A family of the Mahābū clan from Mahābuddha Mandira used to run and maintain the Bāhāḥ including the performance of the daily and anniversary rituals. After this family left the place some time ago, these rituals came to an end." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2400> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2400> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2440> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Thapāḥ Bahī" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2440"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Thapāḥ Bahī is a branch of Guji Bāhāḥ and is located at Thapāḥ Ṭola near to Thapāḥ Hiṭī. A series of steps to the north of the Bahī lead up to its courtyard. There is no gate at the courtyard's entrance, which is unusual for this kind of monument . The god house of this Bahī is very simple, and lacks both a toraṇa above the door as well as the stone lions one usually finds in front of such a structure. A historical photo of the Bahī printed in Pruscha (1975, 196) shows that the god house once had a tiled roof with dormer ( bhaipvāḥ ) on top. However, later photos published in Locke (1985, 198) show CGI sheets covering the roof, and indeed this is still the case. The first floor of the god house has a simple lattice window on its fa&ccedil;ade and an āgamachẽ inside it. The ground floor of the house contains a sanctum with a stone image of an east-facing Akṣobhya. The statue's body is covered with metal cloth ( hẽgā ). A miniature image of this larger one can be found next to it. The god house was badly damaged by the 2015 earthquake and is now supported by wooden columns. A Phalcā containing an image of Mahākāla sits on the northern side of the courtyard. Meanwhile, an Aśoka Caitya marks the centre of the courtyard." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2440> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2440> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2530> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Sum Bāhāḥ (before 1536 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2530"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Sum Bāhāḥ is a branch of Jyo Bāhāḥ ( LAL1770 ). It has a two-storied shrine ( dyaḥchẽ ) which faces west. The roof of the dyaḥchẽ which is supported by the plain struts was originally tiled with the jhĩgaṭīs slates, but it is now covered with the corrugated pewter sheets. [1] The first floor has a trikhā -window flanked by tiki -windows. Also, a sacred place for Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s esoteric deity ( āgama ) is on the second floor. The sanctum ( garbhagṛha ) of the dyaḥchẽ is on its first floor. Previously, a wooden toraṇa on which the images of Akṣobhya (in the middle), and Pra&ntilde;āpāramitā and Lokeśvara (to the right and left of Akṣobhya) was kept above the main door to the dyaḥchẽ (cf. Locke 1985, 77). However, this disappeared or most probably got stolen sometime before 1985 CE. A stone statue of Akṣobhya, flanked by the Sūrya (the god Sun) statue, has been enshrined in the sanctum sanctorum as the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ . [2] A Dharmadhātujinālaṃkṛta Caitya and Dharmadhātu Maṇdala (erected in NS 984, i.e. 1864 CE and NS 1057, i.e. 1937 CE, respectively) are established in the Bāhā&rsquo;s courtyard. [3] Although no documented evidence is so far found to date the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s initial construction, a copy of the document reporting the various events suggests that it must have been built before 1536 CE. [4] When King Raṇa Bahādura (r. 1777-1799) confiscated guṭhī land in the Kathmandu Valley, large portion of guṭhī land of this Bāhāḥ, too, was confiscated. [5] Previously, four Śākya families were involved to perform the daily rituals having endowed two ropanīs of land (from each family). However, only the family of Puṇyarāja Śākya (Śukrarāja Śākya) carries out this task now. The Yogāmbara worship which used to take place every year (on the 10 th of the dark fortnight of Māgha) does not take place anymore in the Bāhāḥ. The dyaḥchẽ was severely damaged during the 2015 earthquakes. The Lalitpur Metropolitan Office has recently allocated 5 lakhs to renovate it. Footnotes ^ This dyaḥchẽ has no pinnacle above its roof. ^ The Akṣobhya statue is dressed in lhegā . ^ Previously, a caitya with a gilded Buddha ( suvarṇa Buddha) statue was erected in the place where the Dharmadhātujinālaṃkṛta Caitya has been erected now. At present, no one knows whereabouts of this caitya . It is believed that this Bāhāḥ was named Suvarṇapura Mahāvirā because of this gilded Buddha statue. ^ This document mentions a dispute from NS 656 (1536 CE) between the members of Sum Bāhāḥ and their neighbor Phyāku Mulmī. ^ Twenty ropanīs of land belonging to the Bāhāḥ was returned to the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s guṭhī in 1862." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2530> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2530> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2550> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Sikucā Bāhāḥ (ca. mid-17th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2550"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Sikucā Bāhāḥ, a branch of Oku Bāhāḥ ( LAL2260 ), is located to the north-east corner of the Thainā crossroad. This bāhāḥ is on the way to Thainā from Saugaḥ Hiti. The bāhāḥ -courtyard is sunken and has an Aśoka Caitya at its center on the axis to the entrance and the main bāhāḥ building. A dharmadhātu maṇḍala is located to the south in front of the caitya . The western wall of the bāhāḥ is attached to a newly constructed five-storied building. Three steps guarded by two small stone lions lead up to the bāhāḥ. Its main entry door is to the north and boasts an original carved door frame with new paneled door leaves. Two small windows on either side of the door are now used as the niches for the māne ( prayer wheel). The ground floor is separated from the first floor by a band of carved timber cornice. The principal fa&ccedil;ade has a central five-fold pāsukājhyāḥ on the first floor; this is flanked by two small aedicules on either side, as is standard. Above the pasukājhyāḥ, one can find seven images of the Buddhist deities with Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā to their right and Lokeśvara to their left. A projecting slanted balcony with a simple railing of vertical balusters fixed to the timber handrail can be found on the second floor. The entrance is flanked by concrete statues of Sāriputra and Maudgalyāyana. Images of the Five Buddhas, parrots, eight auspicious symbols and other motifs have been carved on the entrance. A small caitya flanked by two deers has been fixed above it. Passing through the entrance, one can see a statue of Gaṇeśa to the right and Mahākāla, Garuḍa and Bhīmasena to the left. All are fixed on the walls of the residential buildings. The north-facing three-storied esoteric shrine ( āgama ) of the bāhāḥ stands at the south wing of the courtyard. The pinnacle of the bāhāḥ is a stūpa which is located on the ridge of the corrugated galvanized iron sheets roofing. This roof is supported by plain struts. A metal banner hangs down from the pinnacle to the top of the pāsukājhyāḥ . The first floor houses the āgama, whilst the second floor remains empty. The toraṇa above the left window on the first floor has already been lost. The sanctum of the bāhāḥ is located on the ground floor. J. Locke (1985, 103) mentions that there was once a toraṇa with images of Akṣobhya, Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā and Lokeśvara above the entrance of this sanctum. However, this was stolen some years ago. The entrance is flanked by two concrete images of Lokeśvara and two prayer wheels. The steps leading to the sanctum are flanked by two stone lions and metal flags. Inside the sanctum, one can find a stone image of Akṣobhya. No inscription or other documented evidence with any information about the establishment of the bāhāḥ could be found. A legend suggests that this monastic building's statue of Akṣobhya is related to the Guji Bāhāḥ ( LAL2490 ) . The Guji Bāhāḥ complex once caught fire, and the sandalwood statue of its main deity was brought here for its protection. After the fire, in NS 1 (881 CE), a donor named Vaiśravarṇa reconstructed the bāhāḥ . People then came to this bāhāḥ to retrieve the main deity. However, they could not lift the image up; it had become fixed in its place. As such, the image became siddha (established) there, and so it was that this bāhāḥ got the name 'Siddha Bāhāḥ'. Over time, this changed into Sikucā Bāhāḥ (cf. H. Śākya 2001, 29-30). A tradition dictates that initiates of this bāhāḥ must be taken to Guji Bāhāḥ and shown the image of Akṣobhya. J. Locke (ibid.) mentions that a descendant of Abhayarāja (the man who constructed the Mahābuddha Temple ( LAL2340 )) built the Sikucā Bāhāḥ, though he does not provide any source." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2550> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2550> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2560> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Kvātha Bāhāḥ" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2560"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Kvātha Bāhāḥ is a branch of Oku Bāhāḥ ( LAL2260 ). It is located on the way to Thainā Ṭola from Saugah. The Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s shrine for the principal deity ( dyaḥchẽ ) is not in front of its main east-facing gate but faces north. Unlike other Bāhāḥs, its premises are not in a square shape but in an elongated rectangular shape that stretches from north to south. This was not always the case. The Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s old structure can be seen in the historical photos published by Pruscha (1975,199). It shows intricately designed timber posts on the ground floor of the dyaḥchẽ and equally beautiful trikhājhyāḥ on the first. The statue of the esoteric deity ( āgãdyaḥ ), which used to be located on the first floor, is now stolen. At present, the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s dyaḥchẽ has been completely privatized and is replaced by a residential house. Similarly, the phalcā on the right and left side of the main Bāhāḥ entrance is merged into the private house. It is difficult to ascertain which deity was housed as the Bāhā&rsquo;s principal deity ( kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ ) because of the theft of the kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ&rsquo;s icon. However, the members of the saṅgha mention that the icon of Padmapāṇi Lokeśvara, made with sandalwood, was consecrated as the kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ . Based on this belief, a stone icon of Padmapāṇi Lokeśvara is placed where the old dyaḥchẽ used to be. The icon of Triratnas is placed on top of Padmapāṇi Lokeśvara&rsquo;s statue. Locke (1985, 112), however, mentions that it is Akṣobhya, and not Padmapāṇi Lokeśvara, established as the kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ , and that kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ&rsquo;s icon was mistakenly positioned in the east. There is no inscriptive evidence about the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s construction date and its benefactor. The Sanskrit name of this Bāhāḥ is Kuṭusiṃha Vihāra. Another Bāhāḥ, Jothā Bāhāḥ, is adjacent to the Kvātha Bāhāḥ. The local residents believe that Kuṭusiṃha made this Bāhāḥ after his brother, Jōdhāju, constructed the Jothā Bāhāḥ. There is no independent saṅgha of the Bāhāḥ. Both Jothā Bāhāḥ and Kvātha Bāhāḥ are members of one saṅgha . A single temple caretaker ( dyaḥpāḥlāḥ ) performs the daily worship for both the Bāhāḥs. A Śākya family in Patan, members of this saṅgha , goes by the name of Narsingh Khalaḥ. Anniversary worship ( varṣavardhana ) of both of these Bāhāḥs used to happen on the same day. With the extinction of the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s dyaḥchẽ , the anniversary worship has also vanished. There is a Dharmadhātujinālaya Caitya built in NS 980 (1860 CE) on the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s premises." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2560> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2560> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2590> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Kuladeva Bāhāḥ (built in 1928 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2590"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Kuladeva Bāhāḥ is a branch of Rudravarṇa Mahāvihāra, Oku Bāhāḥ ( LAL2260 ). Unlike many other bāhāḥs or their branches, this Bāhāḥ has no proper kvāḥpāḥchẽ (house for the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ &lsquo;primary deity&rsquo;). Instead, a small temple ( kvāḥpāḥdegaḥ ) made of bricks and plastered in cement, houses a brass statue of Akṣobhya as the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s kvāḥpādyaḥ inside its sanctum . The temple has a caitya shaped pinnacle, and its main entrance to the sanctum is protected with iron bars. It is capped by a toraṇa and is flanked by a Gaṇeśa (left) and a Mahākāla (right) statue. The current brass made Akṣobhya statue is enshrined recently [1] after the previous one made of stone was stolen from the sanctum in 2012 CE (VS 2069). Two brothers, Kuladeva and Caityarāja (supported by Kuladeva&rsquo;s son Mānadeva), had jointly built this Bāhāḥ, installed the aforementioned stone statue of Akṣobhya in the sanctum, and established a Dharmadhātu Manḍala in the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s courtyard in 1928 CE (NS 1048). The Caitya, which has also been erected in the courtyard, is probably older than the Bāhāḥ itself. The bell offered to the Caitya in 1920 CE (NS 1040) by the same two brothers has evidential value for dating. Until recently, the sons of Kuladeva and Caityarāja used to perform the daily rituals in this Bāhāḥ. They have also stopped performing its anniversary rituals which used to take place on the third day of the bright fortnight of Vaiśākha (the Akṣayatṛtīyā day)." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2590> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2590> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2630> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Harṣa Bāhāḥ (built in 1858 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2630"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Harṣa Bāhāḥ is a private Bāhāḥ built by Harṣanarasiṃha Śākya from Haḥkhā Ṭola, Patan in 978 NS (1858 CE). He was a saṅgha member of Kvāḥ Bāhāḥ, commonly known as Hiraṇyavarṇa Mahāvihāra. He did not only built this Bāhāḥ, but also established an esoteric deity ( āgamadevatā ) and Dharmadhātujinālaya Caitya in its premises. The house for the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s principal deity ( kvāḥpāchẽ ) has two stories and faces east. [1] The place for Bāḥā&rsquo;s esoteric deity ( āgama ) and sanctum are located on its first [2] and second floor, respectively. The door to the sanctum is crowned with a toraṇa on which an image of Akṣobhya (in the middle), and Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā and Lokeśvara (on the right and left) have been carved. A stone statue of Akṣobhya is enshrined in the sanctum as the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s primary deity ( kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ ). [3] Further, a Balabhadra statue made of stone is installed in front of the primary deity. Jīvadhanasiṃha (probably a family member of Harṣanarasiṃha Śākya) offered a metal vajra to the Bāhāḥ in 984 NS (1864 CE). Harṣanarasiṃha also established a Dharmadhātu Maṇḍala in the courtyard in 985 NS (1865 CE). The kvāḥpāchẽ was renovated in VS 2049 (1993 CE). Harṣa Bāhāḥ is maintained and run by three families. Each saṅgha member from these three families is responsible to carry out the daily rituals for a month. The Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s anniversary is on every tenth lunar day of the dark fortnight of Vaiśākha. On this day, the annual death rite ( śrāddha ) is also performed to commemorate the deceased elders ( āḥju-aji ). Footnotes ^ Its roof is tiled with the jhĩgaṭī bricks, is capped by a caitya shaped pinnacle and is supported by the plain struts. ^ A trikhā window capped by a wooden toraṇa on which an image of Akṣobhya is carved, can be seen in this floor. ^ The statue faces east." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2630> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2630> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2660> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Trivikrama Viṣṇu Mandira (c. 18th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2660"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Trivikrama Viṣṇu temple is located at Haugaḥ Ṭola, in front of the Haugaḥ Bāhāḥ ( LAL2670 ). This temple is on the left side of the road that runs from Mangal Bazar to Lagankhel. It is built upon a double plinth and the principal fa&ccedil;ade is to the west. The temple can be accessed by climbing a narrow flight of stairs guarded by two small stone lions. The main fa&ccedil;ade has a single carved door on the ground floor and a surmounting toraṇa supported by the door posts. The other three walls have been left blank and have no openings. The first floor level is distinguished by beautifully carved wooden cornice bands. Four carved windows on the first floor are flanked by two small aedicules, and these can be found on each side of the temple. The overhanging roof is covered with traditional jhĩgaṭī tiles and supported by the carved struts. Some of these struts are missing and have been replaced by plain ones. The temple structure's bell-shaped dome structure above the first roof is unique. This towering octagonal dome has been plastered with lime and has a gilded finial with single layer of āmalaka . Sixteen struts support the roof of this two-storied temple. Among them, six older struts display carvings of various incarnations of Viṣṇu. The other, newer struts are plain and were fixed in place after the original ones were stolen. A stone statue of Trivikrama Viṣṇu is enshrined in the sanctum of the temple. This form of Viṣṇu is also called Viṣṇu Vikrānta or Vāmana. Viṣṇu has an aura behind his face and is wearing a crown, earrings, a necklace, arm jewelry and a bracelet. He has eight hands, and holds a sword, a wheel and a punishing stick in three of his right hands. The fourth right hand is performing the gesture of varada. On his left, he holds a shield, a conch and a club, whilst his fourth hand is performing a tarjanī gesture. His left leg rises up to the level of his head. The Śrīmadbhāgavatamahāpurāṇa (8.18) states that Viṣṇu descended as the Vāmana to restore the authority of Indra over the heavens, as it had been taken by the benevolent Asura King Mahābali. Vāmana, a dwarf Brahmin, went to the king to request land that he could set his foot upon for three paces. Mahābali promised to provide him with this amount of land. Vāmana then grew himself to gigantic proportions and strode over the three worlds. With his first step, he covered the space from heaven to earth, and with his second he strode from the earth to the netherworld. WIth these steps, Mahābali's realms were covered entirely, and there was no space for him to take his third step. Unable to fulfill his promise, Mahābali offered his head for the third, and so Vāmana placed his foot on Mahabali's head. No inscription or any other historical document recording the initial construction of the temple could be uncovered. Relying on the architecture of the temple as a dating method, Jośī (VS 2029, 58) assumes that this temple was constructed in the 18th century. Nowadays, the temple is in a neglected condition. There are shops on the temple's plinths, the sanctum is used as a storage room, and the key to the door is in the possession of the shopkeepers." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2660> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2660> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2670> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Haugaḥ Bāhāḥ (built before 1628 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2670"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Haugaḥ Bāhāḥ is located on the southern side of Patan Darbar Square. Unlike other b āhāḥs , Haugaḥ has no affiliation with Śākyas or Vajrācāryas. Rather, it is a private Bāhāḥ belonging to the Rājakarṇikāra community. The Bāhāḥ can be accessed via the carved door located at the east wing of the monastic complex. The entry porch of the complex has four wooden pillars and is suggestive of a colonnaded phalcā with seating space added at a later date. The courtyard measures 10.0m x 9.8m and is encircled by a plinth with a depth of 1.1m. The sunken courtyard is 42cm deep and contains a dharmadhātu maṇḍala and an aśoka caitya . The sanctum building faces east and has a central main door with two stone lions guarding the steps that lead up to it. The door is surmounted by a wooden toraṇa that exhibits three eight-handed tantric deities. The fa&ccedil;ade has a tikijhyāḥ on its first floor and a trikhājhyā on the second floor. The three-storied esoteric building faces east and has a phujā above it. Four decorative struts support the sloping roof, which is clad in brass. The lower roof is covered with corrugated metal sheets and supported by six struts. Four of these display images of gods whilst the other two are decorated with flower motifs. The fa&ccedil;ade of the second floor has a carved window. Glass panes have been fixed in the window frame, and it is flanked by two tikijhyās . The first floor houses the āgama of the Bāhāḥ, and this floor also has a brass-clad trikhājhyāḥ . A brass-clad aedicule has been fixed to the right of the window and a gilded window has been installed to its left. Above the middle window, one can see a wall painting of seven tathāgatas . The sanctum of the Bāhāḥ is on the ground floor. A brass toraṇa exhibiting images of Akṣobhya, Praj&ntilde;āpāramitā and Lokeśvara surmounts the brass-clad entrance of the sanctum. The door is flanked by concrete images of Sāriputra and Maudgalyāyana. An image of Mahākāla can be found to the right of this door, whilst images of Sarasvatī and Hanumān are located to its left, alongside an empty niche that represents the main deity of the āgama . Two stone lions, metal flags and hanging bells have been placed on either side of the entrance. The statue of the main deity of the Bāhāḥ, Akṣobhya, is made of stone and is adorned in lhegā . The building attached to the dyaḥchẽ to the north is used by the Sanāḥ Guṭhī, which is also called Rājakarṇikāra (Halvāī) Śaraṇa Guṭhī. The southern building attached to the sanctum belongs to the Bāhāḥ. A Buddha-statue can be found sleeping on the first floor of this house. The room is named after this statue and is known as 'Relaxing Buddha'. A p a&ntilde;cāvaliyukta caitya and a dharmadhātu maṇḍala have also been placed inside the room. This room is used as an office by the Rājakarṇikāra society. No inscription or any other documented evidence could be found to provide any information about the establishment of the Bāhāḥ. During the Malla period, elephants were reared at the Haugala Ṭola. This area was therefore once known as Hastināgala. The word 'Hastināgala' gradually morphed into variations such as Hātigala, Hatigla, Haugala and Haugaḥ. At that time, one of the ministers of the Malla Kings suggested he bring two Brahmins from Kannauj, India to prepare sweets for him. The king invited these two individuals and offered them land at Hastināgala Ṭola. The Brahmins were called Rājakarṇikāra. They later became integrated into the Buddhist Newar community and established their own bāhāḥ . Later, King Pṛthvīnārāyaṇa Śāha allowed them to open a sweet shop near the shrine of Nārāyaṇa at the present Nārāyaṇahiṭī compound (cf. Locke 1985, 229). A dense population of Karṇikāra caste members surrounds the Haugaḥ Bāhāḥ." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2670> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2670> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2680> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Jom Bāhāḥ" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2680"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Unlike many smaller bāhāḥs in Patan, Jom Bāhāḥ is neither a branch of any major bāhāḥ nor does it belong to the Vajrācāryas or Śākyas. It is a private Bāhāḥ of Śilpakāras ( Lvahaṃkaḥmi ), who are the stone workers. This Bāhāḥ is, in fact, an āgachẽ for the Śilpakāras. Therefore, they prefer to call it āgaṃchẽ , but not Bāhāḥ. Locke (1985, 138-140) and Pruscha (1975, P-268) mention that this is called Jagata Kalyāṇa Vihāra in the Sanskrit language, however, the local residents argue that its Sanskrit name is Viśvakramā Avatāra Vihāra. A Yogāmbara statue is enshrined on the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s first floor inside the sacred and secret shrine ( āgama ). Those who are not initiated ( dīkṣā ) into the tradition are not allowed to enter the āgama . A large window capped by a toraṇa on which an image of Akṣobhya flanked by serpent girls is carved can be seen on the first floor. The Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s sanctum is on the ground floor where an idol of Akṣobhya has been enshrined as kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ . [1] A stone Dharmadhātu Maṇdala [2] is erected in front of the kvāḥpāḥdyaḥ . The door to the sanctum is flanked by Gaṇeśa, Candra, Ma&ntilde;juśrī and Padmapāṇi Lokeśvara (to the right), and Mahākāla and Sūrya (to the left) and is capped by a stone toraṇa on which the images of Pa&ntilde;cabuddhas and Tārā have been carved. A stone statue of Nāmasaṃgīti is kept above the toraṇa . A Śikharakūṭa Caitya, two Dharmadhātu Maṇḍalas, and a statue of Śivapārvatī are established in the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s courtyard. The local residents believe that King of Patan, Siddhinarasiṃha Malla (r. 1620 &ndash; 1661) brought the Śilpakāras and Sthāpitas to Patan in order to make them construct temples and statues etc. in his kingdom. Sometime later, they (Śilpakāras and Sthāpitas) built two āgamas and started living permanently in Patan. Locke (1985, 138) has mentioned a copper-plate inscription recording the date of the Bāhāḥ&rsquo;s initial construction, however, no such inscription is in the Bāhāḥ at present. The earliest reference to this Bāhāḥ can be found on a palm-leaf manuscript from NS 778 (1658 CE). [3] Four Śākya clans (Khicā, Hvakhāḥ, Yākaḥ, and Katilāḥ Khalas) are the members of this Bāhāḥ. Previously, they used to perform the daily rituals turn by turn. However, at present, a Vajrācārya from Mikhā Bāhāḥ has been appointed for such rituals. [4] The Vajrācārya priest worships Yogāmbara enshrined in the āgama every year, on the 14 th lunar day of the bright fortnight of Phāguna. Further, disipūjā is celebrated on the 11 th of the dark fortnight of Pauṣa. Although this Bāhāḥ was initially built by the Śilpakāras, it is also shared by the Sthāpitas. Its digi , too, is divided into two parts. The western part of the Bāhāḥ belongs to the Sthāpitas and its eastern part to the Śilpakāras. Footnotes ^ The Akṣobhya statue is dressed in a metal lhegā . ^ The date NS 795 (1675 CE) is inscribed on the Dharmadhātu (see the object LAL2680-011 below). ^ The manuscript records the name of a person (Siddhi Bhāro) who is a member of Oṃ (text: Jom) Bāhāḥ (cf. Rājavaṃśī 2041, 75). ^ He receives a monthly enumeration of 3500 rupees." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2680> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2680> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2720> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Ibāḥ Bahī Dune (19th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2720"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Ibāḥ Bahī Dune Bāhāḥ is a branch of the Ibāḥ Bahī ( LAL2730 ) and is situated to its west. This Bāhāḥ can be accessed through the back door of the Ibāḥ Bahī. Alternatively, there are also other two access points: either from the southwest corner, or the northwest corner from Ibāḥ Bahī. Bhājudhana Siṃha from Ibāḥ Bahī constructed this small Bāhāḥ, and so it is also called Bhājudhanasiṃha Vihāra. The courtyard measures 4 m &times; 13 m and contains a shrine of Akṣobhya Buddha at its centre. The god house is attached to the western wall of the Ibāḥ Bahī, and measures 3.2 m in width and 2.1 m in depth. It is built in brick masonry and has a flat concrete slab as a roof. The main door of the structure is not centrally aligned with the shrine, and it lacks the artistic features common to Bāhāḥ. The door has some carvings, and is flanked by wall paintings of Sāriputra and Maudgalyāyana. A small window is fixed to the left-hand side of the main door. Inside the sanctum, there is a north-facing brass image of Akṣobhya. The stone image of the deity was stolen 3-4 years ago, and this new one was installed in its place. On top of the roof, one can find a stūpa -shaped finial. Images of Hanumān and Mahākāla are attached to the wall and flank the entrance that leads to Ibāḥ Bahī. The Bāhāḥ-courtyard is paved with teliyā bricks and also contains a Pa&ntilde;cāvaliyukta Caitya. No inscription or other document providing information about the establishment of the Bāhāḥ could be found. However, Pruscha (1975, 204) dates it to the 19th century." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2720> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2720> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2740> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Nārāyaṇa Mandira at Cāka Bāhāḥ (ca. 1699 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2740"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "The single-storey Nārāyaṇa temple has a domed roof and is located on the eastern side of the road that runs north-south from Mangal Bazar to Lagankhel. The temple stands on a double plinth and can be accessed via stone steps to its west. The main arched entry is also to the west; blind stone doors can be found on the other three sides of the temple. There is a multi-layered terracotta motif above the entry level. A stone nāgo and floral ( libī ) motifl can be found almost half way up the temple, in between the layer and the base. All the doors are topped with kīrtimukha toraṇas. A stone image of Śrīdhara Viṣṇu stands in the sanctum in a samapāda posture, holding śaṅkha and cakra in his two right hands, and gadā and padma in his left hands. Images of Lakṣmī and Garuḍa have been sculpted on the same stone. Lakṣmī stands to the left of Viṣṇu in tribhaṅga. Her left hand is shown performing a gesture of abhaya whilst her right hand performs varada . Garuḍa stands to Viṣṇu's right in samapāda, and is in a praying posture. Images of Śrīdhara Viṣṇu usually show Lakṣmī to his right and Garuḍa on his left. However, in this statue their position has been inverted. In addition to this main statue, there is also a smaller and partially damaged image of Viṣṇu in the sanctum. This has been placed inside a niche. The rectangular base of the bell-shaped dome has a number of niches containing idols of Lakṣmī and Nārāyaṇa. These can be found on all four sides. A bronze pinnacle sits on top of the temple. Recently (in 2012), a wind vane was fixed next to the pinnacle, alongside statues of Viṣṇu's weapons. An oil-lamp balustrade with 108 lamps surrounds the temple, while a bell donated on the same date hangs on a beam besides the lower plinth at its southwest corner. A pillar with an image showing Garuḍa sitting and praying to Śrīdhara Viṣṇu was also erected in 2012. No inscription mentioning the exact date of the establishment of the temple could be found. However, an astrologer called Śivanārāyaṇa installed a stone inscription dated NS 819 inside the temple. This stone records the names of his predecessors, and provides grounds to hypothesise that either he or one of them established the temple. Inside the sanctum, another broken inscription dated NS 754 informs readers of the renovation of a phalcā , which suggests that this inscription was brought here from somewhere else. The daily and annual ritual worships of this temple are no longer performed. The Cakravarti Ṭola Sudhāra Samiti organises the opening and closing of the temple." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2740> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2740> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2750> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Umāmaheśvara Mandira at Cāka Bāhāḥ" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL2750"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "The Umāmaheśvara temple is located in Cāka Bāhāḥ Ṭola and stands on a three-tier plinth. It has a composite roof type which combines a tiered roof and a dome. Before 1934 earthquake, the temple had three tiers and was constructed in the style of a pagoda. However, it was levelled by the earthquake and reconstructed in this mixed style. This temple is located approximately 8 m east of the Nārāyaṇa Temple ( LAL2740 ), and together they form a complex with streets passing by to the west and south. Residential houses surround the square, which has now almost turned into a market place; street vendors occupy most of the open space. The sanctum of the temple can be accessed via stone steps to the west. Two stone lions sit on the first plinth level to guard the deities inside. At the southwest corner of the plinth, one can find a hanging bell that was donated in 2012. A statue of Nandī (vehicle of Śiva) is in front of the temple, and a trident has been erected close to this icon. The entrance to the sanctum is clad in metal, and has a toraṇa with an image of Garuḍa on top and Umā-Maheśvara at the centre, flanked by Gaṇeśa and Kumāra. The roofs as well as the columns are clad in copper. On top of the roof, one can see a pinnacle alongside a metal trident. A serpent covers the top of the pinnacle, while four other snakes creep up to the tip of the second roof from each of the bottom four corners. The main sanctum is a square room with doorways on all four sides. Inside, one can find a four-faced śivaliṅga clad in brass. Of the four doors, the western door is the main entry way. Surmounted by a torana , the entryway is encased in carved metal sheets and has a raised stone threshold (29 cm), while the other doors are much more simple and plain. A stone statue of Umāmaheśvara is attached to the eastern wall, and the sanctum also contains images of Śrīdevī, Bhūdevī, Viṣṇu and Sūrya (sitting on a chariot pulled by seven horses). The image of Sūrya (the Sun god) is a recreation of the archaic one, which was stolen some decades ago. Pruscha (1975 , 209) mentions that there was once an image of Candramā (the Moon god) here, though there is no other proof of this. The stone inscription attached above the southern door of the temple mentions that the monument was reconstructed by Viśvambhara Bhāro in NS 796 (CE 1676) during the reign of King Śrīnivāsa Malla. The inscription further informs readers that the King and his son Yoganarendra Malla were also present at the consecration of the temple, where lands were bestowed and a guṭhī was established to fund its ritual worship However, these days no guṭhī lands connected to the temple could be traced. In VS 2058, local people collected donations in order to try to continue the daily and other ritual worships of the temple. They assigned a Brahmin from Agniśālā Ṭola to carry out this task. However, the earthquake 2015 further hindered the daily worship. The Cakravarti Ṭola Sudhāra Samiti (an organisation for local development) is currently responsible for opening and closing the temple." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL2750> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL2750> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL4093> a hg:DhungeDhara ;
    rdfs:label "Vajradhara Phalcā (ca. 19th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL4093"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "The small courtyard in which Vajradhara Phalcā is located is called Ghana Bāhāḥ ( LAL2330 ) and contains a small temple in which a new Akṣobhya Buddha image resides. The temple is flanked by two phalcās: Mahākāla Phalcā ( LAL4092 ) to the south-east and Vajradhara Phalcā to the north-west. Vajradhara Phalcā used to house a Vajradhara statue, however this was stolen around 1985. J. Locke (1985, 112) confirms that the shrine was empty upon his visit. Since the disappearance of the deity, religious activities at the Phalcā have also ceased. The stone stele located behind the stone pedestal has three lines inscribed in Sanskrit. The walls and niches were once plastered. Currently, the Phalcā is used for storing building materials for a house under construction next door. Only its stone pedestal remains.Vajradhara Phalcā is in an advanced state of decay, though it retains many of its original components such as simple carved pillars, a traditional shrine with a historic carved door, and its jingati roof tiles. However, the ridged tiles have been replaced with plain brick and the roof is leaky. The platform&rsquo;s stone edge on the eastern side appears to have been well-preserved but the tile paving is badly damaged. The original entry door at the east has been replaced by a frame covered in metal." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL4093> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL4093> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL4109> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Dabū Phalcā (1704 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL4109"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This free-standing three-bay Phalcā faces east and is located in the north of Thati courtyard. Dabū Phalcā contains carved wooden pillars ( dhaliṃkhvāḥ) and small windows which can be dated back to the 17th century. Six old columns with kalasa motifs, a capital showing a dragon, small windows and rare panels have been preserved. The Phalcā's roof is covered with jhĩgaṭī tiles and has been recently repaired by a Khaḍgī at his own cost. Inside, one can find two inscriptions and a statue of Padmapāṇi Lokeśvara. The chariot of Matsyendranātha stops at the Thati Ṭola for several days, where it rests in the public space between this and two other phalcās . One of the inscriptions in this Phalcā mentions that it was constructed in 1704 CE (NS 824) by Narahari Bhāro and his wife Vasumuni, residents of Dhopogatra (present Dhaugala ṭola) during the reign of Yoganarendra Malla. The same inscription mentions the construction of a stage platform ( dabū ), though this has since been removed. The second inscription states that Siddhivajra Vajrācārya from Hiraṇyavarṇa Mahāvihāra recited Guṇakāraṇyavyūha at the chariot procession of Matsyendranātha in 1868 CE (NS 988). The inscription does not mention whether the recitation took place inside the Phalcā or somewhere else. The building was reconstructed one month after this recitation took place. A representative of each of the four major Vihāras of Patan was involved in both the recitation and the reconstruction process. No religious or cultural activities are observed here any longer." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL4109> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL4109> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL4154> a hg:Stupa ;
    rdfs:label "Cyāsal Phalchā 4" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL4154"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This Phalchā is located at the eastern part of the Cyāsal square next to Aśoka Stūpa Phalcā ( LAL4155 ). The whole building was rebuilt in concrete some time in the 1990s." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL4154> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:StupaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL4154> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL4161> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Nāsaḥ Phalcā" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL4161"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Nāsaḥ Phalcā is a single-storey Phalcā situated in Cyāsal. It stands on the western edge of the route which leads from from Kvaylāchi (south) to the outskirts of Cyāsal (north), and its rear wall is attached to the Nāsaḥ Capāḥ. From the Phalcā, one enters into an office that is located inside but otherwise disconnected from the building of the Capāḥ, which is dedicated to Nāsaḥdhyaḥ, the god of music and dance. The first floor of the Capāḥ is located above Nāsaḥ Phalcā and is used for Hari bhajan. It was reconstructed in 2018 after being dismantled and rebuilt in 1992. None of the historic elements were preserved. The structure now has a cement roof with modern columns. Its associated groups are the Nāsaḥ Guṭhī, the Cyāsaḥ Maṃkā Khalaḥ and the Yanyā Guthi of Bya&ntilde;jankārs of Nhukaṃchẽnani (Mahārānī Cukaḥ)." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL4161> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL4161> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL4165> a hg:BuddhistMonument ;
    rdfs:label "Yākaḥ Phalcā (ca. 18th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL4165"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Yākaḥ Phalcā is located to the south of Gaṇeśa Mandira ( LAL0157 ) in Om Bāhāḥ Ṭola. The Phalcā is open to the north and west, with just one bay on each side. A single-bayed structure is called y ākaḥ in Newari and the Phalcā is named accordingly. The Phalcā has three carved columns and six struts. The roof is sloping down to the northern and western sides. There is an empty niche on the eastern wall of the Phalcā. According to local residents, the previous Phalcā building had a niche on the same the wall of the same direction and there were statues of Nārāyaṇa and Umāmaheśvara in the niches, which were lost before some decades. A small Gaṇeśa shrine stands next to Yākaḥ Phalcā, towards the south. It is still worshipped. No historical record is available to date the Phalcā. However, according to the local Śreṣṭha community made it around 250 years before. In the 1934 earthquakes, the building collapsed. It was then reconstructed (n.d.). In 1989, local residents demolished the crumbling structure and in 2019, the Lalitpur Municipality reconstructed it with the help of seven lakh Nepali rupees. An electric power pole stands behind the Phalcā and quasi-encroached the building. Every year on the 11th day of the bright fortnight of the Bhādra, an image of Yamāḥdyaḥ (Indra) is exhibited on top of an erected pole and on the 13th day, samaybaji (a ritual snack) is distributed. On the 4th day of the dark fortnight of Āśvina, the images are taken down. In earlier times, similar six images of Yamāḥdyaḥ were exhibited." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL4165> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL4165> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL4201> a hg:DhungeDhara ;
    rdfs:label "Tāpā Hiti Sataḥ" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/LAL4201"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "The two-storey Sataḥ faces towards the east. No historical record is available to date the building. Villagers from Tamang and Magar communities would stay overnight in this Sataḥ when they came to the Valley to sell their local products, though this no longer seems to be the case. Ekatā Paṭhaśālā was established in the building during the Rana period. Afterwards, the building was used by the Ganesh Youth Club, and the first floor of the building was also used by the Ṭola Sudhāra Samiti. The office was moved to a new building constructed on top of the filled in pond to the north of Tāpā Hiti Phalcā East ( LAL4199 ). This likely occured some time in the 2010s. At present, the ground floor of the Sataḥ is used to store old materials. The sataḥ building has been deteriorating over time and was further damaged by the earthquakes of 2015. The inner walls of the building have cracked in many places." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/LAL4201> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/LAL4201> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/PPK1610> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Gopāleśvara Mandira" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/PPK1610"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "The temple known as Gopāleśvara houses a śivaliṅga and is situated on the cremation site of a sage named Gopāla Muni. The temple features a dome-shaped structure and faces south, located on the path between Sūryaghāṭa and Kailāśa. It has a central finial on top of the dome, and although there were originally smaller finials on the four corners of the roof, only two remain (as of June 2023). The temple's substructure is made of brick and contains the sanctum where a four-faced ( caturmukhī ) śivaliṅga is enshrined as the main deity. East of the temple is the Sūryaghāṭa Śivālaya ( PPK1612 ) ensemble. Inside the sanctum, there is a statue of Hanumān on the western wall, and a square-shaped hollow space designated for fire oblations. The temple has entrances on all four sides with brick arches ( toraṇa ). The exact date of the temple's construction is unclear, but its architectural style suggests that it was built during the Rāṇā period. The śivaliṅga inside the sanctum was installed around 2022 CE by devotees. The temple premises were neglected and unclean for many decades until devotees took the initiative to clean and develop it into a place suitable for meditation. Unfortunately, the temple was already in a dilapidated state when it was further damaged by the earthquakes in 2015. No priest is formally appointed for the temple. Instead, a person from the Nepālī caste has been taking care of the temple for the past 25 years. Due to its serene location, the temple area has become a popular spot for meditation, and the number of people visiting for this purpose has increased over the years. On the auspicious occasion of Śivarātrī, which falls on the 14th day of the dark fortnight of Phālguna, a bonfire is set up in front of the temple. Other socio-religious activities are not regularly conducted at this temple." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/PPK1610> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/PPK1610> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/SUN0030> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Dampāchẽ Caityas (1979 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/SUN0030"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Two dharmadhātujinālaya caityas , commonly referred to as chaturamukha caitya (i.e a caitya which has four faces) and a dharmadhātu maṇḍala are located on the western side of the main road at Hitipusaḥ Jaḥrũ, Dampāchẽ, Sunāguthi. Four Buddha images sit on top of the upward opening lotus blossoms above a single layer of petals. These blossoms are supported by a lion throne positioned on top of a square pedestal which in turn sits upon the square plinth. A drum, a dome and a finial have been placed above the four Buddha images. The caityas are 200 cm in height. An octagonal shaped dharmadh ātu ma ṇḍala (80 cm in height) lies to their west." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/SUN0030> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/SUN0030> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/SUN0103> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Pañcāvalīyukta Caitya (1863 CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/SUN0103"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "This caitya displays five layers of motifs (lotus, jewel, water, fire and thunderbolt) upon its upper part, and contains images of four Buddhas and four Tārās in the four cardinal directions and corners respectively. The seats of the deities are clad in brass, as are the toraṇa above them. These images are tied to iron rods to protect them against theft. The caitya 's dome ( garbha ) and harmikā have been joined together using cement plaster, and the finial is already broken. Statues of the 13 donors are located on the southern face of vedikā alongside an inscription that records the construction date of the caitya ." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/SUN0103> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/SUN0103> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/SUN0200> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Mahākāla Shrine (ca. early 20th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/SUN0200"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "During special occasions such as jātrā and other festivities, the residents of Sunaguthi are supposed to worship four religious sites in the Sunaguthi area. This Mahākāla shrine is one of those four sites. On such occasions, long lines of devotees stand and queue in front of the shrine. The shrine contains a statue of Mahākāla (38 &times; 27 cm) and is located inside the south-facing shikhara -style temple. Mahākāla is standing on top of a corpse and holding kartikā , kapāl a and kha ṭvāṅga . A square stone pillar with a lotus pedestal on top of it stands to the west of the structure. This pillar was erected in 1951 (VS 2008) by Guru Kavi Prasada after he organised the saptāha bhajana in Sunaguthi. Next to it, a wooden post supports a small bell bearing an inscription from VS 2031 (1974 CE). A copper plate inscription can be found on the western wall of the shrine and states that the statue was restored in 2001 CE (VS 2058)." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/SUN0200> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/SUN0200> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/SUN0210> a hg:Chaitya ;
    rdfs:label "Vajradhātu Caitya (ca. 19th cent. CE)" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/SUN0210"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Local residents refer to this Caitya as Ta ḥ Ga ḥ Bhaw āndya ḥ. According to H. Śākya&rsquo;s (1993, 82) classification, this Caitya is a Vajradhātu Caitya. The Caitya has niches on its eastern, southern, western and northern sides which contain stone statues of Akṣobhaya, Ratnasambhava, Amitābha and Amogasiddhi respectively. The stone inscription attached to the western platform has been severely damaged. The Caitya has a gu ṭhī that consists of five members from the Jyapū community. They have been taking care of the monument and conduct its yearly religious activities." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/SUN0210> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_architectural_style hg:ChaityaStyle ;
