<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/SUN0250> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Capĩgāḥ" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/SUN0250"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Local residents still remember the open water channel [ deydha ḥ ( desadhala)] that used to run through Sunāguthi. A small pit ( kuṇḍa ) called Capĩgāḥ is located on the bank of this water channel and was once used to collect water. This water was considered to be holy and was offered to the statue of Bālakūmāri located in the temple to the east ( SUN0230 ). A local religious song also mentions bathing in the Capĩgāḥ. The royal water channel ( rājakulo ) dates back to the 9th century and used to supply water from Lele to Patan and the villages along its route. A road widening project undertaken in 2012 necessisted the closing of the formerly open water channel and the water was diverted into underground cement pipes. The Capĩgāḥ was filled with rubble, and the current water tank made of brick and cement was constructed in its place. This tank remains empty and appears to be neglected. According to local informants, the tank is filled with water only once a year, on the full moon day of Caitra during the celebration of a jātrā . When the procession cots stop in front of the Bālakūmāri Temple, water from the Capĩgāḥ is sprinkled on the cots. This Capĩgāḥ stands as an example of tangible and intangible heritage that has been severely affected by modern construction work." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/SUN0250> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/SUN0250> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/SUN4225> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Gãdyaḥ Phalcā" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/SUN4225"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "The G&atilde;dyaḥ Phalcā is located at Pukhusi, Sunaguthi (widely known as Sunakothi) at the junction where a street leading towards Kulimha curves away from the Sunāguthi main road. No evidence could so far be traced with which to date the initial construction of this Phalcā. The photo published in Pruscha 1975 (vol. 1), 99 shows the building's original structure before it was rebuilt in 1995 due to damage suffered by its earlier five-bayed structure. The reconstruction saw the addition of a blank masonry wall to the rear and eastern sides. The Phalcā is open to the south and west directions, though it is protected by a grill. Two small metal doors are fixed to the southern wall. The foundation stone of the old structure can be seen on the plinth, and some of the salvaged roof tiles are stacked on top of the roof slab. The Phalcā has socio-cultural importance for Patan. The masks of the Navadurgās from Thecva are stored here during the Mohanī festival before they are taken to way to (and return from) a performance at Mūla Coka ( LAL1260 ) at Patan Darbar Square. Local residents come here to worship the masks and take part in a procession around Sunaguthi. The procession is followed by a dance performance in front of Kvaylāchī Phalcā ( SUN4200 )." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/SUN4225> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/SUN4225> .

<https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/SUN4230> a hg:ArchitecturalStructure ;
    rdfs:label "Gaṇeśa Phalcā 2" ;
    dct:identifier "https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/danam/SUN4230"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Gaṇeśa Phalcā 2 is located to the south of Gaṇeśa Mandira ( SUN0102 ) and to the east of Bhṛṅgāreśvara Mandira ( SUN0100 ). This one-storey RCC Phalcā is adjacent to the eastern entrance of the Bhṛṅgāreśvara temple complex. Before it was dismantled around 1986, the Phalcā had a gable roof with tiles. This roof was then replaced by a concrete slab supported by three RCC columns during its reconstruction. The rear brick masonry wall made from mud mortar and stones on the plinth are the only traces of the original structure that remain. Dāphā Bhajana is performed inside the Phalcā, and so it is also known as Dāphā Phalcā. The monument finds most use during festivals." ;
    crm:P55_has_current_location <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/place/danam/SUN4230> ;
    prov:wasInfluencedBy hgsrc:danam ;
    hg:existence_status hg:Extant ;
    hg:has_provenance_assertion <https://data.cair-nepal.org/heritagegraph/id/assertion/danam/SUN4230> .

